WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.290
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:09.000
Welcome to the Castle Lecture.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.703
It's so wonderful to
see so many of you here.

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.470
Before I introduce our speaker
today, Kathleen Thelen,

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:17.820
I want to tell you a little bit

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.310
about this Castle Lecture series.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.800
These are the signature speaker series

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.830
of the ethics, politics,
and economics major

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.320
at Yale University.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.600
These lectures aim to promote awareness

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.470
of the ethical challenges and
complexities of societies,

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:37.860
and they're also meant
to foster robust dialogue

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.550
about the moral foundations
of society and government.

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.250
The series was established in 1988,

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.820
when Mr. Castle made a
generous gift to Yale

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.910
in honor of his ancestor,
Reverend James Pierpont,

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:55.910
who founded the college at
school of Connecticut in 1701.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:57.750
This name might be familiar

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.520
because this school then became renamed

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.520
after its main benefactor,
Yale University.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.470
So we're grateful to Mr.
Castle and his family

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:11.870
for the contribution to EP&amp;E

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.120
and to Yale College more generally.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.180
Our speaker today is Kathleen Thelen,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.330
Ford Professor of
Political Science at MIT.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.210
Her work focuses on the
origins and evolution

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:28.260
of political economic
institutions in rich democracies,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.210
and she's the author among others

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.430
of Varieties of Liberalization

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:35.490
and the New Politics of Social Solidarity,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.200
How Institutions Evolve,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:40.710
and she's co-editor of
American Political Economy:

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:43.380
Politics, Markets, and Power.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.170
During her career she
has received many awards

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.180
for her insightful work.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:49.740
And because of time constraints,

00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.570
I'm not gonna go through the whole list,

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.050
I'm just gonna mention a
few of the most recent ones.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.100
She was awarded

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.000
the Aaron Wildavsky
Enduring Contribution Prize,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.130
the Michael Endres Research Prize,

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:04.530
the Barrington Moore Book Prize,

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:07.620
the Woodrow Wilson
Foundation Award of the APSR

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.720
among many others.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.580
She holds honorary degrees

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:14.637
at the Free University of Amsterdam, LSC,

00:02:14.637 --> 00:02:18.180
the European University
Institute in Florence,

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.283
and the University of Copenhagen.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.430
Professor Thelen has served as President

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.790
of the American Political
Science Association.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:31.200
She has also been Chair of the
Council for European Studies

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.960
and the President of the
Society for the Advancement

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.880
of Social Economics.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.830
She was awarded, sorry,

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.920
she was elected to the American
Academy of Arts and Sciences

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:44.610
and to the Berlin-Brandenburg
Academy of Sciences.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.950
Finally, she's a key figure

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:50.553
in the Consortium of
American Political Economy,

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.400
recent network of scholars

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.190
that examine the United
States' distinctive mix

00:02:56.190 --> 00:02:58.800
of capitalism and democracy.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.950
And fitting with the consortium
mission, in today's talk,

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.040
we're gonna hear about
American retail capitalism

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.070
and the origins of the Amazon economy.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:09.750
The format of this lecture

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.400
will be that Professor Thelen

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.290
will speak for about 50 minutes,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.170
and then we're gonna open up for questions

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.040
from the audience.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.083
So please join me in
welcoming Professor Thelen.

00:03:20.083 --> 00:03:23.083
(audience applauds)

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.520
- So I wanna first begin by saying

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:30.930
that this is a tremendous honor

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:34.800
to have been asked to
deliver the Castle Lecture.

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:39.000
I wanna thank my Yale
colleagues for the invitation.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.820
I wanna thank John Castle and his family,

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:43.533
in absentia unfortunately,

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:48.570
for sponsoring and
supporting this whole series

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:52.860
and all of you for coming today to listen.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:57.450
So I became interested in
American retail capitalism

00:03:57.450 --> 00:03:58.890
as I was watching this kind

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:02.640
of ferociously ambitious
retail entrepreneur

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:05.940
who was engaged in a
really aggressive strategy

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:08.130
of relentless expansion,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:11.070
offering convenient at-home shopping

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:15.030
for a kind of ever
widening range of products,

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:18.090
while also maintaining very low prices

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:22.770
because of this huge sales
volume that this was generating.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:27.770
So this company's success,
of course, provoked antipathy

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.600
among the small independent merchants

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.050
who really couldn't match
the sort of large inventory

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:37.470
and especially low cost
of this retail giant.

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.480
You also had critics complaining

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.310
that this company's profits

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.980
were being underwritten by taxpayers

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.010
because of the central role
that the US Postal System

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.953
was playing in delivering
these goods to customers.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.700
Meanwhile, the firm's employees,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.070
virtually all of them
of course un-unionized,

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.380
were bearing the brunt of
this really monotonous,

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:01.800
but also kind of frantically paced work

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.080
in state-of-the-art warehouses

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.263
deploying the latest technologies.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:11.310
Suppliers to this company felt squeezed

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.670
and trapped in a relationship
of unequal dependence, right?

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:16.320
They were relying on this outlet

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.230
to get their goods to their customers.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.230
But customers, well,
customers were smitten

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:26.670
because now they had the
luxury of shopping from home,

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.980
choosing from this really wide selection

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:33.980
of every imaginable product
all at unbeatable prices,

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:36.120
with money back guarantees

00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:39.180
and special perks for loyal customers,

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.630
and everything delivered
right to their door.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:45.720
And if you think that
I'm talking about Amazon,

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.640
you would actually be wrong

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.440
because the company that I'm describing

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.823
is actually Sears and Roebuck circa 1908.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.460
So it was these parallels

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:59.880
between the business
strategies of Amazon today

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.640
and those of Sears over a century ago

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.470
that really sparked my interest

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:08.700
in retail capitalism in the United States.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.540
Okay, so I'm a comparativist,
I'm a Europeanist,

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.570
but the American case is really important

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.550
for students of the political economy

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:19.800
of the rich democracies, like myself,

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.300
because the US is really seen

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.410
as the kind of quintessential
consumer society.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:29.070
Consumption and, by extension, retailing

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:32.160
is just widely recognized
as absolutely critical

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:37.160
to sustaining this country's
demand-driven growth model.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.960
So it turns out that
retailing and distribution

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.330
is a substantively very important sector

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:44.970
across really all the rich democracies.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:48.270
It accounts for between 10 and 15%

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:51.360
of jobs across really
all the OECD countries,

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:54.543
which actually puts it very
close to manufacturing.

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:58.050
It's even higher in the United States.

00:06:58.050 --> 00:07:01.620
As you see, in 2003, the
number of Americans working

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.620
in retail actually
surpassed the number working

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:05.453
in factories.

00:07:05.453 --> 00:07:07.500
And in the last couple of decades,

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.360
retail really passed manufacturing.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:13.200
It's kind of the leading
force in economic growth

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.390
in the United States.

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.340
So in terms of employment,

00:07:17.340 --> 00:07:20.550
large discount retailers specifically

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:23.010
are, by really big margins,

00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:26.580
the biggest employers
in the United States.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.080
And big retailers, as you see here,

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.480
really just don't play the
same sort of central role

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:32.850
in employment in other countries.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.520
And even though European retailing

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.430
has been kind of moving
toward the American model

00:07:38.430 --> 00:07:41.130
on some dimensions, I
think it's fair to say

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:45.093
that the US really still stands
out in some important ways.

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:48.630
First off, as you see in this figure,

00:07:48.630 --> 00:07:50.190
as I hope you can see,

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:54.120
the US just has way more
retail space per capita

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:54.993
than Europe.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:57.270
And that's a function, of course,

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.970
of these huge shopping
centers that you find

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.740
in most American suburbs.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:03.420
What you see on the right here

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.840
is the famous Mall of
America in Minnesota,

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:11.840
which has over 500 stores and
over 20,000 parking spots.

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:15.930
But the outsized presence
of these retailers

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:20.730
is also a function of these
big box discount retailers

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:24.450
like Target and Walmart
that are really ubiquitous

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:25.470
in the United States.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.370
According to Forbes, 90%
of Americans actually live

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:32.820
within 15 minutes of a Walmart.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:36.420
And of course these places
are basically always open.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:38.850
So one of the biggest
differences to Europe

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:42.480
is that shopping hours are
simply far more regulated

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.610
in most European countries.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:46.470
And then last but not least,

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:48.750
these huge low cost retailers

00:08:48.750 --> 00:08:52.140
are actually really popular
in the United States.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:54.990
So this is, on the left
is the results of a survey

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:57.900
from 2019, which shows that, you know,

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:00.930
Amazon counted as
America's best loved brand.

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:05.070
And as you see, a lot of
these other big risk retailers

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:06.750
are not very far behind.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:09.720
On the right, I confess that's
not the most scientific study

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:12.420
in the world, but basically when asked,

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.820
you know, which companies they love,

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:17.553
usually Germans are gonna
go for their car companies.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:22.320
So the central role played by retail

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.180
and these big discount retailers,

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:26.910
specifically within the US,

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:29.520
seemed to be one of the
most distinctive features

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:31.680
of the American political economy.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.760
And so I set out to try to
explain how we got here.

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:39.690
So what do we know about
the politics of retailing?

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:41.340
There's really a lot

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:45.090
of, I would say, wonderful
work on consumer culture

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:46.750
and on consumer credit

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.600
mostly by economic historians,
also by sociologists,

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.100
but almost all of this work

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:58.680
is focused on a particular slice of time,

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.380
namely the post World War II period.

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:03.840
And most of this work, or
almost none of this work,

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:06.480
is on retail, it's on
consumption and consumers,

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:09.813
but not on retail specifically,
which is my focus.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.710
So economists and management scholars

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.450
who have studied retail

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:19.080
have tended towards sort
of apolitical explanations

00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:21.990
for the success of these
mega retailers, right?

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.470
It's a story about the size
of the American market,

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:29.010
about technological or
organizational innovations

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.830
on the part of these kind
of brilliant entrepreneurs

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:36.030
that allowed these companies
to grow to dominance.

00:10:36.030 --> 00:10:38.040
Chandler would be kind
of the classic on this,

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:42.240
but there are also more recent
examples of this as well.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.030
So, unsurprisingly, I'm
a political scientist.

00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:48.540
The argument that I've
developed is more political.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.660
I wanna argue that mass
retailers in the United States

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:55.140
were not simply responding
to market opportunities.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:56.790
They were creating them,

00:10:56.790 --> 00:11:00.570
and they were creating them
by exploiting regulatory gaps

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:02.793
as they grew in scale and scope.

00:11:04.140 --> 00:11:05.670
So legal scholars

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:08.490
have offered a somewhat
more political version

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:11.190
of the story of the rise
of these big retailers.

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:14.520
So there's, of course, this
seminal article by Lina Khan,

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:17.010
who is well known here at Yale,

00:11:17.010 --> 00:11:18.990
and who is now, of course, as you know,

00:11:18.990 --> 00:11:20.850
the head of the Federal Trade Commission.

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.820
And she attributed the rise of Amazon,

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:25.920
in particular to the ascendants

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.700
of the so-called Chicago
School of Law and Economics

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:33.150
starting basically in the
'60s and especially '70s.

00:11:33.150 --> 00:11:37.740
And this is a school of thought
that put consumer welfare,

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.470
defined essentially as price,

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.710
at the center of American antitrust theory

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:44.790
and jurisprudence.

00:11:44.790 --> 00:11:47.140
There's a couple of spots
up here, if you like.

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:58.200
So clearly the rise of the Chicago school

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.330
has been very important.

00:12:00.330 --> 00:12:02.010
And as you're gonna hear,

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:05.070
antitrust actually plays a really big role

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:08.130
in the explanation that
I've been developing.

00:12:08.130 --> 00:12:11.160
But where I part ways
with at least that strand

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:12.690
of legal scholarship,

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:15.720
is that I wanna show that
the changes of the 1960s

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:18.300
are actually really
just the latest chapter

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:21.330
in what is in fact a much longer story,

00:12:21.330 --> 00:12:25.320
one in which large retailers
have basically always enjoyed,

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.780
an extraordinarily privileged position

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:30.093
within the American antitrust regime.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.750
So finally, comparative
political economists,

00:12:33.750 --> 00:12:37.410
for their part, really
haven't had that much at all

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.870
to say about the politics of retail

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:41.940
or distribution generally.

00:12:41.940 --> 00:12:44.040
The focus of almost all of this work,

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:48.210
and I include my own, no,
I include my own here,

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:51.993
is just overwhelmingly on
the politics of production.

00:12:52.920 --> 00:12:54.000
So to the extent

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.610
that comparative political
economists like myself

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:58.530
have addressed these questions,

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:01.410
it's a literature that focuses not so much

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:05.160
on the politics of retailing
but on consumer credit

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.250
and especially the
expansion of consumer credit

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:11.670
in the US in response
to the Great Depression.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:14.130
Again, this is clearly important,

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:17.610
but what I wanna argue is
that the focus on credit

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:19.770
has really tended to obscure the role

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:22.620
of these large discount retailers

00:13:22.620 --> 00:13:25.230
that always stayed away from credit sales,

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:27.480
but that I think have had a huge impact,

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:31.110
not just on the American retail landscape,

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:34.500
but on the American
political economy as a whole.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:36.780
So this project traces the origins

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:40.080
of what I'm calling the Amazon
economy all the way back

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:42.330
to the late 19th century.

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:45.210
This is a period when these
large scale retailers begin

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:47.310
to capitalize on what I see

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:51.720
as a uniquely permissive
regulatory landscape in the US

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.880
to basically outgrow the capacity

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:55.923
of the government to regulate them.

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:00.150
So two features of the American
political economy figure

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:02.250
especially prominently in the explanation

00:14:02.250 --> 00:14:03.723
that I've been developing.

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.590
The first is the role of
the American judiciary

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:10.500
and an antitrust regime
that, as I wanna say,

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:15.090
from the beginning, gave mass
retailers enormous advantages

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:18.630
and in ways that were
completely distinct from Europe.

00:14:18.630 --> 00:14:21.210
And second is regulatory fragmentation.

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:24.690
And here I basically mean a
political economic landscape

00:14:24.690 --> 00:14:27.480
that assigned most of
the regulatory powers

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.370
to state and local governments,

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:33.240
which turned out to be no match
for these large retailers,

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:36.060
who, you know, based not
least on the head start

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:37.920
that they had received from the courts,

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:41.460
were quickly operating
on a national scale.

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:45.240
So the argument in a nutshell
is that judicial forbearance

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:49.200
and regulatory arbitrage
allowed large American retailers

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:50.400
to grow.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:51.930
And as they grew, they were able

00:14:51.930 --> 00:14:55.830
to win over an ever expanding
political support coalition

00:14:55.830 --> 00:14:57.960
of groups who, over time,

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:02.130
came to rely on these big
companies in various ways.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:05.760
And then of course, these
groups could then be weaponized

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:07.410
to defend these companies

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:10.803
in their political battles
with would-be regulators.

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:15.210
So this is a study that
tries to cover more

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:17.220
than 100 years of retail development.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:18.180
I'm not gonna give you a sort

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:21.750
of blow by blow
comprehensive treatment here,

00:15:21.750 --> 00:15:24.710
but instead what I wanna do
in this talk is just zoom in

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:28.560
on a couple of the key
junctures on the screen here,

00:15:28.560 --> 00:15:30.480
where these dynamics played out

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:31.800
and where, as I wanna show,

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:35.520
the politics unfolded very
differently from Europe.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:37.410
So the first period, if you will,

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:39.600
is the construction of the mass market

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:40.950
in the late 19th century.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.080
This is a period where
these mail order retailers

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:45.600
played an enormously important role.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:48.660
The second is the growth
of mass consumption.

00:15:48.660 --> 00:15:50.400
In the 1920s, this is the period

00:15:50.400 --> 00:15:54.630
that saw really the explosion
of chain store retailing.

00:15:54.630 --> 00:15:57.120
And the third period is the
post World War II period

00:15:57.120 --> 00:15:58.890
that saw the rise to dominance

00:15:58.890 --> 00:16:01.533
of these big box discount retailers.

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.740
So circling back,

00:16:04.740 --> 00:16:07.050
starting with the
construction of a mass market.

00:16:07.050 --> 00:16:09.060
So, you know, in the late 19th century,

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:10.410
there was no mass market,

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.930
not in Europe, not in the United States.

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:14.820
Basically what you had everywhere

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:18.240
is merchants scattered all over the place.

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.330
Each of these merchants
carried very limited selection,

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:22.830
to say the least.

00:16:22.830 --> 00:16:25.230
They charged very high prices,

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:26.790
partly because they were at the end

00:16:26.790 --> 00:16:29.130
of very long supply chains,

00:16:29.130 --> 00:16:31.890
but also partly because each
of them, of course, operated

00:16:31.890 --> 00:16:35.793
as a kind of local monopoly
within their little area.

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:40.290
So in Europe, consumer co-ops emerged

00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:42.420
to challenge these small merchants,

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:46.020
and they grew to become
the first mass retailers.

00:16:46.020 --> 00:16:49.380
So you'd have farmers or
workers who would join together

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:52.320
to pool resources in
order to get better prices

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.090
by purchasing in high volume

00:16:54.090 --> 00:16:57.060
and then sell directly to members.

00:16:57.060 --> 00:17:00.090
These local cooperatives
would then scale up

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:03.690
by developing ties to
cooperatives in other regions,

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:06.180
sort of the other localities and regions,

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:08.430
sort of growing from bottom up and out

00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:10.140
until they grew to national,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:12.960
in some cases international scale.

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:15.240
So this was happening throughout Europe,

00:17:15.240 --> 00:17:18.540
most famously, I would
say, in Scandinavia,

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:21.780
where cooperatives are
actually still major players

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:24.060
in the retail landscape.

00:17:24.060 --> 00:17:27.510
But actually the earliest
and most successful example

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:31.530
of this emerged in what is
America's closest cousin,

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:32.643
namely Britain.

00:17:33.510 --> 00:17:37.200
And this took the form of the
cooperative wholesale society,

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:39.720
which grew out of these
local cooperatives,

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:43.680
but that really took off
after legislation in the 1850s

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:47.460
and especially '60s, allowed
individual cooperatives

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:50.100
to coordinate their activities, okay?

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:52.980
And so that is what
allowed for the formation

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:57.870
of a national society, a
co-op of co-ops, if you will,

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.840
that became, you know, grew to
become the biggest retailer,

00:18:00.840 --> 00:18:03.300
not just in Britain, but in the world

00:18:03.300 --> 00:18:05.430
by the end of the 19th century.

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:07.170
And then as in other countries,

00:18:07.170 --> 00:18:10.080
this national cooperative association

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:13.020
in Britain also then
began integrating backward

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.140
in order to start
producing some of the goods

00:18:16.140 --> 00:18:16.973
that they sold.

00:18:16.973 --> 00:18:18.790
And so what you see on the right here

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:22.113
is one of their production facilities.

00:18:23.340 --> 00:18:25.260
So similar cooperative ventures

00:18:25.260 --> 00:18:27.960
were also getting founded in the US

00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:29.760
in the late 19th century.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.760
So some of you of course will
know about the Grange movement

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:35.370
and its successor the, Farmer's Alliance.

00:18:35.370 --> 00:18:37.860
These were important in American history

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:41.520
as the main organizations
that farmers developed

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:43.440
to advance both their economic interests,

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:46.590
but also to channel their
political grievances.

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:48.780
And these were, of course, associations

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:52.200
that became hugely important
in the populist revolt

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:55.920
against monopoly power
in the late 19th century.

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:58.230
So starting in the 1860s,

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:00.930
these local Grange chapters
organize themselves

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:05.130
into cooperatives, both to
try to secure higher prices

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:08.820
for their own goods, but
also to try to pool orders

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:13.820
to secure lower costs for their
members on consumer goods.

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:19.020
Unlike in Europe though, these
efforts were not really met

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:21.780
by state support or even forbearance,

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:24.510
in fact, even as cooperatives in Europe

00:19:24.510 --> 00:19:26.700
were really flourishing in this period.

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:29.610
They failed to thrive and
grow in the United States.

00:19:29.610 --> 00:19:32.250
And one of the bigger
obstacles that they faced

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:35.190
was this fragmented and fractured

00:19:35.190 --> 00:19:38.310
and far less congenial legal landscape.

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:40.470
So it's a complicated story,

00:19:40.470 --> 00:19:43.620
but the short version of the
story is that unlike in Europe,

00:19:43.620 --> 00:19:47.250
there was no national legislation
in the late 19th century

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:51.420
that could clarify the legal
standing of these cooperatives,

00:19:51.420 --> 00:19:54.720
and that expressly
sanctioned their activities.

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:57.000
This was getting decided in the courts.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:00.570
And while courts in some
states saw no problem

00:20:00.570 --> 00:20:01.950
with these cooperatives,

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:03.900
especially starting in the 1880s,

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:06.060
courts in other states began

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:08.460
to view the kind of joint marketing

00:20:08.460 --> 00:20:11.670
and distribution that
really stood at the center

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:14.460
of the cooperative
project as price fixing,

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:15.990
and thus running afoul

00:20:15.990 --> 00:20:19.050
of common law prohibitions
against conspiracies

00:20:19.050 --> 00:20:20.880
in restraint of trade.

00:20:20.880 --> 00:20:23.070
So at this point, sometimes
state legislatures

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:24.660
would step in and pass a law trying

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:27.450
to exempt these cooperatives
from prosecution,

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:31.050
but federal courts often took
a pretty hard line on this

00:20:31.050 --> 00:20:33.570
and struck down some of these state laws

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:37.770
as unconstitutional, either
on grounds of equal protection

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:41.250
or after the Sherman
Antitrust Act of 1890,

00:20:41.250 --> 00:20:44.250
as violations of federal antitrust laws.

00:20:44.250 --> 00:20:46.380
And this is of course paradoxical

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:48.450
because these agrarian interests

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:51.270
had provided a great deal of the impetus

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:53.550
behind the Sherman Antitrust Act,

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:56.790
which was supposed to be
directed against the JP Morgans

00:20:56.790 --> 00:20:58.110
and Rockefellers of the world,

00:20:58.110 --> 00:21:00.420
but which often came to be deployed

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:03.273
against their own
cooperative associations.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:06.870
So the failure of those ventures

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:11.250
then provided a huge opening
for private corporations

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:13.380
to basically move into this space,

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:15.000
because individual companies

00:21:15.000 --> 00:21:16.710
that organized all these marketing

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:19.200
and distribution functions internally

00:21:19.200 --> 00:21:22.830
were of course outside the
scope of these antitrust laws.

00:21:22.830 --> 00:21:24.750
There's no collusion, no conspiracy

00:21:24.750 --> 00:21:29.370
if you're within a single
private entity or company.

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:31.830
So Montgomery Ward was
really the first one

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:34.770
to kind of open the vast consumer markets

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:37.260
of the American West and South,

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.990
but it was this guy, Richard Warren Sears,

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:43.200
who really came to dominate this market.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:44.220
And so he moved in

00:21:44.220 --> 00:21:46.560
with an extraordinarily
aggressive strategy

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:48.720
of very high volume sales,

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:51.960
very thin margins, very low prices,

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:56.220
and on a really insanely
wide range of products.

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:59.100
So when it was founded in 1886,

00:21:59.100 --> 00:22:01.290
the Sears catalog, if you
wanna call it a catalog,

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:03.420
was just a one page flyer.

00:22:03.420 --> 00:22:05.673
All he sold was watches.

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:09.870
But within just 20
years, just two decades,

00:22:09.870 --> 00:22:12.630
this catalog was 1,000s of pages long.

00:22:12.630 --> 00:22:15.300
You could literally buy
everything from toothbrushes

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:18.210
to entire prefabricated houses

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:21.330
that would come to you
with all the millwork done

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:23.190
and, you know, sort of instructions

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.200
for how to reassemble them, right?

00:22:25.200 --> 00:22:28.350
So it's sort of like
ordering an IKEA chair

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:29.250
or something like that.

00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:32.430
Some of these Sears houses
are actually still standing

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:33.570
in the American Midwest.

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:36.393
Many of them are in
Illinois, you can visit them.

00:22:37.290 --> 00:22:40.680
So the expansion of these
companies across the US

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:42.510
would not have been possible

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:45.450
had it not been for the
way in which they were able

00:22:45.450 --> 00:22:48.900
to exploit existing public infrastructure

00:22:48.900 --> 00:22:50.430
for private purposes.

00:22:50.430 --> 00:22:52.380
And here the US Postal System

00:22:52.380 --> 00:22:54.810
played an absolutely critical role

00:22:54.810 --> 00:22:57.450
because this is what
provided these companies,

00:22:57.450 --> 00:23:01.620
not just with a subsidized
means of getting their catalogs

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:05.700
and their goods to this widely
dispersed rural population,

00:23:05.700 --> 00:23:09.240
but also, I would say,
much more importantly,

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:11.580
local post offices were able to serve

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:16.580
as a critical intermediary
in the financial transactions

00:23:16.710 --> 00:23:19.563
that these companies were
having with their customers.

00:23:20.520 --> 00:23:23.460
So, as I said, in Europe,
these cooperatives

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:26.520
had grown kind of from the
bottom up and out, right,

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:27.960
through Federation.

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:30.240
So they had local branches
all over the place.

00:23:30.240 --> 00:23:32.610
They could sell things for cash

00:23:32.610 --> 00:23:35.310
or they could let people run a tab.

00:23:35.310 --> 00:23:37.170
Montgomery Ward and Sears, of course,

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:40.290
by definition did not have retail outlets.

00:23:40.290 --> 00:23:42.330
And moreover, virtually all

00:23:42.330 --> 00:23:44.700
of their rural customers were unbanked.

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:45.810
They had no bank accounts,

00:23:45.810 --> 00:23:49.650
they were operating basically
on a cash or barter economy.

00:23:49.650 --> 00:23:53.430
So how are you going to do
these long distance sales?

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:56.400
Well, you could of course
send cash through the mail,

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:57.540
but it, which as you know,

00:23:57.540 --> 00:23:59.400
if you've seen American Westerns,

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:01.500
stage coaches were constantly getting,

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:03.150
you know, that were carrying the mail

00:24:03.150 --> 00:24:04.590
were constantly getting held up,

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:08.100
which is why this guy has
his hands up in the air.

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:11.220
So none of this actually
would have been possible

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:13.200
without the money order system

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:16.020
that the US Postal system offered.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:17.220
So this was a service

00:24:17.220 --> 00:24:19.860
that the government
had introduced earlier,

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.410
and for completely different reasons,

00:24:22.410 --> 00:24:26.970
in the Civil War, in order to
allow union soldiers basically

00:24:26.970 --> 00:24:30.153
to get their money back home safely.

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:34.710
But these retailers were able
to repurpose this service

00:24:34.710 --> 00:24:37.830
to serve as a critical
financial intermediary

00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:40.950
in their transactions with customers.

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:42.330
And just to give you a sense

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.580
of how much a company
like Sears, for example,

00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:48.690
depended on the infrastructure
of the US Postal System,

00:24:48.690 --> 00:24:52.710
here are the instructions from
a Sears catalog from 1908.

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:55.650
It says, "if you live
on a rural mail route,

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:59.400
just give the letter and the
money to the mail carrier

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:02.400
and he will get the money
order at the post office

00:25:02.400 --> 00:25:05.160
and mail it in the letter for you."

00:25:05.160 --> 00:25:08.130
So this is how America's
first mass retailers

00:25:08.130 --> 00:25:12.120
were able to reach and win
over millions of customers

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:15.750
who now had access to this unlimited range

00:25:15.750 --> 00:25:18.120
of standardized consumer goods

00:25:18.120 --> 00:25:22.950
and at prices their local
merchants could not hope to match.

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:24.930
And once they had this loyal,

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:27.870
I would say, more or less
captive consumer base,

00:25:27.870 --> 00:25:30.090
they could also mobilize them

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:33.660
as a powerful ally in
their political battles.

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:36.360
And one of the most
important of those battles

00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:40.830
was to secure legislation
to expand postal services

00:25:40.830 --> 00:25:43.590
to carry more and bigger packages.

00:25:43.590 --> 00:25:44.883
So parcel post.

00:25:45.930 --> 00:25:49.290
This is something that's
obviously of clear value to Sears,

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:53.310
but very much opposed both
by the big express companies

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:57.540
that you otherwise had to pay
to transport all those goods

00:25:57.540 --> 00:26:00.210
and buy the small merchants that Sears

00:26:00.210 --> 00:26:03.240
was, of course, putting out
of business at this time.

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:06.960
So when legislation on parcel
post was being considered,

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:11.160
mass retailers actively
weaponize their rural consumers

00:26:11.160 --> 00:26:13.650
among other ways, encouraging them

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:15.810
to basically blanket Congress

00:26:15.810 --> 00:26:17.727
with petitions, which they did.

00:26:17.727 --> 00:26:19.740
And so what you see on the right here

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:24.060
is that petitions in favor
of parcel post spiked

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:25.500
at precisely the moments

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:28.320
when Congress was considering legislation

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:29.460
to fund this service.

00:26:29.460 --> 00:26:33.180
And those were coming
from exactly these areas.

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:35.340
So when it came time for the vote,

00:26:35.340 --> 00:26:37.530
virtually all of the support

00:26:37.530 --> 00:26:41.160
that carried this legislation
came from Democrats

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:42.720
in the rural south and west,

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:44.580
which is what you see
in the dark blue here.

00:26:44.580 --> 00:26:49.260
These were exactly the areas
that relied virtually entirely

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:50.850
on mail order companies

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:53.673
in order to link them
into this national market.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:58.380
Parcel posts then ushered
in a kind of golden age

00:26:58.380 --> 00:26:59.970
of mail order.

00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:02.880
Sears saw its sales increase fivefold

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:06.033
in just the first year of parcel post.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:10.290
Montgomery Ward, as you
see, was not far behind.

00:27:10.290 --> 00:27:12.630
So in this first period,

00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:15.000
these large mail order companies

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:17.370
enjoyed extremely favorable treatment

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:19.140
under American antitrust,

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:21.930
they proceeded to grow by exploiting

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:26.010
and repurposing public
infrastructure for private ends.

00:27:26.010 --> 00:27:27.630
And American farmers, of course,

00:27:27.630 --> 00:27:30.150
who otherwise were battling big business,

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:31.650
they did not battle these companies.

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:33.750
They in fact welcomed them with open arms

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:35.610
because it liberated them

00:27:35.610 --> 00:27:38.670
from these high priced local markets,

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:42.723
bringing them a kind of world
of exotic new consumer goods.

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:47.310
This then sets the scene
for the explosive growth

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:51.420
of these chain stores in
the early 20th century.

00:27:51.420 --> 00:27:54.270
So the early 20th century
and the inner war period,

00:27:54.270 --> 00:27:57.990
the progressive era and
the so-called roaring '20s,

00:27:57.990 --> 00:28:02.850
is this really paradoxical
period in American history,

00:28:02.850 --> 00:28:05.760
one that was characterized simultaneously

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:10.170
by high and rising inequality,
both wealth inequality,

00:28:10.170 --> 00:28:13.170
which is in the blue
or kind of green here,

00:28:13.170 --> 00:28:15.360
and income inequality.

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:18.360
But it was also a period
that saw a real boom

00:28:18.360 --> 00:28:21.150
in consumption and consumer spending.

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:24.300
So Americans were buying
things like washing machines

00:28:24.300 --> 00:28:26.160
and vacuum cleaners really decades

00:28:26.160 --> 00:28:29.490
before their counterparts in Europe.

00:28:29.490 --> 00:28:33.600
So how did the average
American get all this stuff?

00:28:33.600 --> 00:28:36.660
Part of the answer is that
established retailers,

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:38.730
which by now of course includes Sears,

00:28:38.730 --> 00:28:41.850
they were already
selling things on credit.

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:45.060
And that's another story
of regulatory arbitrage

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:47.190
that I try to tell in the book.

00:28:47.190 --> 00:28:49.800
Basically the expansion of consumer credit

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:53.370
in this period expanded
really in a legal gray zone,

00:28:53.370 --> 00:28:56.523
very much sort of under the radar.

00:28:58.560 --> 00:29:01.410
And I can talk more about
that in the Q&amp;A, if you like.

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:04.530
The other development though,
that fueled consumption

00:29:04.530 --> 00:29:08.130
in the 1920s was the
really phenomenal growth

00:29:08.130 --> 00:29:12.510
of chain stores which operated
on a cash only basis, okay?

00:29:12.510 --> 00:29:15.360
So you had new entrants
to the retail sector,

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:18.180
Woolworths, JCPenney, Ben Franklin.

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:20.370
They did not offer credit.

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:23.850
They were competing exclusively on price.

00:29:23.850 --> 00:29:27.780
So these chains grew
extraordinarily rapidly

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:29.070
in just these 10 years.

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:30.180
So just 10 years.

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:32.640
I put some figures on the
screen just to give you a sense

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:36.843
of the explosion of these
chain stores in this period.

00:29:36.843 --> 00:29:39.090
What you also see at
the bottom of the screen

00:29:39.090 --> 00:29:41.850
is that the numbers in
Europe are much lower.

00:29:41.850 --> 00:29:44.250
So where variety chains
already were accounting

00:29:44.250 --> 00:29:48.270
for nearly a quarter of
retail turnover in the US,

00:29:48.270 --> 00:29:51.330
they were only accounting
for 7% in England

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:54.393
and under 2% in other
countries on the continent.

00:29:55.560 --> 00:29:59.970
And this was in large part
because these kinds of operations

00:29:59.970 --> 00:30:04.970
were encountering much
stiffer headwinds in Europe.

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:06.930
In other words, the politics

00:30:06.930 --> 00:30:09.360
were, again, playing out very differently

00:30:09.360 --> 00:30:11.760
on the other side of the Atlantic.

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:15.150
So these conflicts centered on two issues.

00:30:15.150 --> 00:30:17.700
One was resale price
maintenance arrangements

00:30:17.700 --> 00:30:20.760
and the other was special
taxes on large retailers.

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:23.370
And I just wanna talk
briefly about each of these

00:30:23.370 --> 00:30:25.860
in order to underscore the differences

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:28.080
to Europe at the time.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:31.410
So resale price maintenance
arrangements involve contracts

00:30:31.410 --> 00:30:33.930
through which manufacturers
commit retailers

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:38.310
to charge a specific, usually
minimum price for their goods.

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:41.520
So this is promoted by
manufacturers of patented goods

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:44.340
who wanna protect the
reputation of their brands,

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:47.670
but, of course, small retailers
also love these arrangements

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:50.700
because if everybody had
to charge the same price,

00:30:50.700 --> 00:30:52.230
this would relieve them

00:30:52.230 --> 00:30:55.170
of price competition from these larger,

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:57.270
more efficient producers.

00:30:57.270 --> 00:31:00.660
So in that sense, resale price
maintenance kind of reflected

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:03.150
or maybe created a kind of alliance

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:04.950
between brand manufacturers

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:06.990
and small merchants, on one hand,

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:11.280
against these larger low cost
retailers who at the time

00:31:11.280 --> 00:31:14.040
were already advocating specifically,

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:17.010
explicitly the right for
what they call the right

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:18.183
to cut prices.

00:31:19.530 --> 00:31:23.280
So resale price maintenance
contracts were perfectly legal

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:27.000
and indeed widespread in
Europe throughout this period,

00:31:27.000 --> 00:31:28.080
throughout the interwar period

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:33.000
and indeed well into the
post World War II period.

00:31:33.000 --> 00:31:37.170
Mostly they survived until the 1960s.

00:31:37.170 --> 00:31:39.030
In Germany, resale price maintenance

00:31:39.030 --> 00:31:41.940
was only banned in 1974.

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:44.490
And this too goes back to
this different approach

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:47.880
to competition policy,
because unlike in the US,

00:31:47.880 --> 00:31:50.490
courts in Europe in the early 20th century

00:31:50.490 --> 00:31:54.330
were adhering to the principle
of freedom of contract

00:31:54.330 --> 00:31:57.900
but including recognizing contracts

00:31:57.900 --> 00:31:59.100
that, under American law,

00:31:59.100 --> 00:32:01.353
would've been considered anti-competitive.

00:32:02.730 --> 00:32:06.720
So resale price maintenance
arrangements were not just legal

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:09.540
in European coordinated market economies,

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:12.570
this you might expect,
but also in Britain,

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:15.600
again, a fellow liberal market economy

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:20.460
and also one that shares
America's common law tradition.

00:32:20.460 --> 00:32:22.050
So the organizational vehicle

00:32:22.050 --> 00:32:24.690
through which these arrangements operated

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:28.980
was the British Proprietary
Articles Trade Association.

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:31.440
This was founded in 1896,

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:34.530
and this was an outfit that
brought together manufacturers,

00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:38.790
wholesalers, and retailers
who jointly administered

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:43.790
and policed a list of protected
prices on trademarked goods.

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:45.090
So what I'm about to describe

00:32:45.090 --> 00:32:47.370
would've been 100% illegal in the US,

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:48.690
but the way it worked

00:32:48.690 --> 00:32:51.930
is that manufacturers set their prices,

00:32:51.930 --> 00:32:54.930
retailers would then monitor those prices,

00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:57.150
they would notify the association

00:32:57.150 --> 00:33:00.420
if some retailer was found to
be undercutting that price,

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:02.070
and then the association

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:05.340
would collectively enforce
the manufacturer's price

00:33:05.340 --> 00:33:08.370
by putting that retailer
on a so-called stop list.

00:33:08.370 --> 00:33:09.660
And being on the stop list,

00:33:09.660 --> 00:33:12.720
then obliged all other association members

00:33:12.720 --> 00:33:15.040
to stop supplying that firm

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:17.970
or that retailer.

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:21.180
So small independent retailers
were beneficiaries of this

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:23.640
because this channeled retail competition

00:33:23.640 --> 00:33:26.100
in Britain away from price

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:28.950
and toward competition centering more

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:32.190
on quality and on service.

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:33.870
So the situation in the US

00:33:33.870 --> 00:33:36.060
was radically different.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:37.350
From very early on,

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:41.550
starting at the latest in
1911, the Supreme Court ruled

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:44.010
that resale price maintenance arrangements

00:33:44.010 --> 00:33:47.610
were, per se, illegal
under prevailing antitrust.

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:49.290
And the landmark case here

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:53.940
was one that pitted a
large discount drugstore,

00:33:53.940 --> 00:33:57.510
a price cutter, against
a small manufacturer

00:33:57.510 --> 00:33:59.430
of patented medicine.

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:02.130
And in this case, the court ruled

00:34:02.130 --> 00:34:03.750
in favor of the big drug chain,

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:06.930
basically giving retailers control

00:34:06.930 --> 00:34:08.733
over the prices they charged.

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:12.720
So this case was controversial.

00:34:12.720 --> 00:34:16.290
It inspired the formation of
a so-called Fair Trade League

00:34:16.290 --> 00:34:18.900
that advocated changes in antitrust law

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:21.750
to try to allow smaller firms

00:34:21.750 --> 00:34:24.480
to engage in greater coordination,

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:26.700
to protect themselves from domination

00:34:26.700 --> 00:34:29.013
by these larger entities,

00:34:30.240 --> 00:34:33.450
especially to steer
competition away from price

00:34:33.450 --> 00:34:36.750
and toward innovation and quality.

00:34:36.750 --> 00:34:40.230
So the Fair Trade League had
some very prominent advocates,

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:43.860
including future Supreme
Court Justice Louis Brandeis,

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:45.180
among others.

00:34:45.180 --> 00:34:47.970
And Brandeis was actually well aware

00:34:47.970 --> 00:34:49.560
of what was happening in Europe,

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:51.960
and he really admired some of the aspects

00:34:51.960 --> 00:34:53.520
of the alternative model

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:56.340
of what he called regulated competition

00:34:56.340 --> 00:34:59.580
that he saw developing there at the time.

00:34:59.580 --> 00:35:01.740
So he and other progressives saw this ban

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:03.390
on resale price maintenance

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:07.380
as really just encouraging
ruinous price competition.

00:35:07.380 --> 00:35:10.050
He called it competition that kills.

00:35:10.050 --> 00:35:11.970
This would inspire a race to the bottom,

00:35:11.970 --> 00:35:13.440
it would lead to monopoly,

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:15.960
especially because it would kill off all

00:35:15.960 --> 00:35:18.813
of these smaller innovative competitors.

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:23.010
But American Free Traders
were really quite stymied

00:35:23.010 --> 00:35:26.010
in the courts really throughout the 1920s.

00:35:26.010 --> 00:35:29.130
In other words, precisely
through that period,

00:35:29.130 --> 00:35:30.540
which as I just pointed out,

00:35:30.540 --> 00:35:31.980
was when these chain stores

00:35:31.980 --> 00:35:35.670
were experiencing their
most explosive growth.

00:35:35.670 --> 00:35:37.650
So the situation changed a little bit

00:35:37.650 --> 00:35:39.780
in the early 1930s.

00:35:39.780 --> 00:35:41.400
Now we're in the midst
of the Great Depression.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:44.190
Brandeis is, by this time,
of course on the bench,

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:46.440
and the Supreme Court did relax

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:50.280
what rules states could pass in this area.

00:35:50.280 --> 00:35:52.860
And some states, in fact many states,

00:35:52.860 --> 00:35:56.220
did pass fair trade legislation.

00:35:56.220 --> 00:35:58.350
But very different from Europe,

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:01.260
brand manufacturers
could still not legally

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:03.210
and openly enlist the help

00:36:03.210 --> 00:36:06.300
of other retailers or other producers

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:09.000
in defending the prices that they set.

00:36:09.000 --> 00:36:10.890
That would still be illegal.

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:14.730
So enforcement ran entirely
through private litigation.

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:18.480
And private litigation was
extraordinarily expensive

00:36:18.480 --> 00:36:21.870
for most firms, and it was
certainly too expensive

00:36:21.870 --> 00:36:24.390
for most of the small firms that Brandeis

00:36:24.390 --> 00:36:26.940
and others had really sought to protect.

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:28.770
Just to give you one
example, at one point,

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:32.760
General Electric was pursuing
3000 different lawsuits

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:36.390
against various companies
for violating its contracts,

00:36:36.390 --> 00:36:37.980
which is a huge legal expense

00:36:37.980 --> 00:36:39.903
these small companies couldn't match.

00:36:41.010 --> 00:36:44.100
Moreover, state laws, of course,

00:36:44.100 --> 00:36:46.650
only cover intra-state trade.

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:49.590
So this did not really stop these chains

00:36:49.590 --> 00:36:52.530
that had now grown to national scale

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:55.350
and that could engage
in regulatory arbitrage,

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:57.840
basically switching up their sourcing

00:36:57.840 --> 00:37:00.060
in order to avoid fair trade states

00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:05.010
or just buying the entire
output of a single supplier.

00:37:05.010 --> 00:37:07.800
So chain store age, which was

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:11.250
and still is actually the
main chain store magazine,

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:13.680
was constantly publishing tips

00:37:13.680 --> 00:37:16.560
on how to work around these laws.

00:37:16.560 --> 00:37:20.763
So basically too little
and arguably too late.

00:37:22.320 --> 00:37:25.350
Running parallel to all of these battles

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:27.990
over resale price maintenance
during the depression

00:37:27.990 --> 00:37:31.920
were other efforts to slow
down the chains in other ways.

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:34.230
And this took the form of efforts

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:36.600
to impose heavy taxes on chain stores.

00:37:36.600 --> 00:37:39.660
This is, again, something
that small retailers

00:37:39.660 --> 00:37:40.647
would've loved to see.

00:37:40.647 --> 00:37:42.630
And so what you have on the screen here

00:37:42.630 --> 00:37:45.210
is just to kind of sample
of some of the rhetoric

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:46.290
of the time.

00:37:46.290 --> 00:37:48.690
These guys are, you know,
vicious price cutters.

00:37:48.690 --> 00:37:51.780
They're running legitimate
retailers out of business.

00:37:51.780 --> 00:37:53.640
They're callous foreign entities

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:58.080
that don't contribute
anything to the local economy.

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:00.660
So a lot of these campaigns
against the chains

00:38:00.660 --> 00:38:04.860
were really pretty successful,
particularly in the south

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:06.990
and, yeah, south and west.

00:38:06.990 --> 00:38:09.720
And this is because this
was coming at a time

00:38:09.720 --> 00:38:12.720
when these small variety
chains were moving in

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:16.890
and really placing a lot of
pressure on local businesses,

00:38:16.890 --> 00:38:20.040
businesses that were in
the depression, of course,

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:22.893
already suffering from the downturn.

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:25.800
It's also the depression.

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:30.300
And so populism is enjoying
a renaissance at this point.

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:32.490
You have people, you
know, Wall Street bankers

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:34.590
are being blamed for the depression.

00:38:34.590 --> 00:38:37.477
You have populace like Huey Long saying,

00:38:37.477 --> 00:38:40.020
"I would rather have thieves and gangsters

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:42.810
than chain stores in Louisiana."

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:46.530
So in fact chain store
taxes did get passed

00:38:46.530 --> 00:38:49.890
in many of these, or in some
of these states at least.

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:53.070
But again, they ran into
trouble in the courts,

00:38:53.070 --> 00:38:56.070
certainly in the 1920s,
when chain store taxes

00:38:56.070 --> 00:38:59.130
were getting struck down, again,
on equal protection grounds

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:00.780
under the 14th Amendment.

00:39:00.780 --> 00:39:04.230
And even when these taxes
were not getting struck down,

00:39:04.230 --> 00:39:06.840
chains were just engaging
in the same sorts

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:08.550
of regulatory arbitrage,

00:39:08.550 --> 00:39:11.130
sort of reorganizing their operations

00:39:11.130 --> 00:39:12.930
in order to avoid these taxes

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:15.753
or to minimize their
obligations under them.

00:39:17.010 --> 00:39:21.420
So state level regulation
was clearly ineffective.

00:39:21.420 --> 00:39:26.420
And so this all culminated
in 1938 in one big,

00:39:26.580 --> 00:39:28.710
I would say, kind of last ditch effort

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:32.490
to impose a chain store
tax at the national level.

00:39:32.490 --> 00:39:36.660
And that was an effort that
was spearheaded by this guy,

00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:38.670
Texas Representative Wright Patman,

00:39:38.670 --> 00:39:42.870
who some people have called
America's last populist.

00:39:42.870 --> 00:39:47.070
But his proposal for this
national chain store tax

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:48.750
really went down in flames

00:39:48.750 --> 00:39:50.700
because by this time,

00:39:50.700 --> 00:39:53.190
chains were able to mobilize their allies

00:39:53.190 --> 00:39:56.340
in huge public relations campaigns.

00:39:56.340 --> 00:39:59.070
So they did things like sponsor referenda

00:39:59.070 --> 00:40:00.840
in order to mobilize voters.

00:40:00.840 --> 00:40:04.080
And so on the right here, you
see just an example of this.

00:40:04.080 --> 00:40:06.420
This is from a referendum in Utah,

00:40:06.420 --> 00:40:08.670
where basically the chains are saying,

00:40:08.670 --> 00:40:10.230
you know, if you put taxes on us

00:40:10.230 --> 00:40:11.850
or if you raise taxes on us,

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:16.113
that's simply going to result
in higher prices for you.

00:40:17.190 --> 00:40:19.110
So by the time things,

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:21.180
you know, by the time it came time

00:40:21.180 --> 00:40:23.220
to consider Patman's legislation,

00:40:23.220 --> 00:40:26.730
a whole bunch of groups kind
of came out of the woodwork

00:40:26.730 --> 00:40:28.050
to defend the chains.

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:31.890
So you had farmers and farm
organizations turning out

00:40:31.890 --> 00:40:35.250
in the congressional
hearings to defend the chains

00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:37.890
because in the meantime,
they had come to depend

00:40:37.890 --> 00:40:41.310
on these grocery chains
as an efficient way

00:40:41.310 --> 00:40:43.080
to distribute their products

00:40:43.080 --> 00:40:47.010
and also especially to
unload periodic surpluses,

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:50.670
which was of course a big
problem for American farmers.

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:53.250
You had manufacturers,
even the manufacturers

00:40:53.250 --> 00:40:56.430
who had previously fought
the chains on prices,

00:40:56.430 --> 00:40:58.500
now they climbed on board

00:40:58.500 --> 00:41:01.260
because they were, of course,
extremely capital intensive.

00:41:01.260 --> 00:41:04.800
And so now this economic
downturn had sort of taught them

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:09.450
to appreciate the kind
of predict predictability

00:41:09.450 --> 00:41:12.360
that these large retailers provided

00:41:12.360 --> 00:41:15.750
because they ordered in very large volume

00:41:15.750 --> 00:41:17.103
and well in advance.

00:41:18.030 --> 00:41:20.640
And finally, you know,
consumers were basically hooked

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:21.780
on the chains.

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:25.920
Consumers in the US have
historically been represented

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:28.380
by women's clubs, by women's groups.

00:41:28.380 --> 00:41:30.720
And so women's groups also turned out

00:41:30.720 --> 00:41:33.540
in the congressional
hearings to defend the chains

00:41:33.540 --> 00:41:36.573
as a kind of go-to source
for low cost goods.

00:41:37.530 --> 00:41:40.290
So by the late 1930s,

00:41:40.290 --> 00:41:44.010
already just a few years
after it had gotten going,

00:41:44.010 --> 00:41:48.450
this chain store taxation
movement was now kind of spent.

00:41:48.450 --> 00:41:52.500
Now you had states repealing
their chain store taxes.

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:55.380
Now in fact you had states often competing

00:41:55.380 --> 00:41:58.320
to have these lower cost retailers locate

00:41:58.320 --> 00:41:59.580
in their jurisdictions.

00:41:59.580 --> 00:42:01.770
And so this is an article

00:42:01.770 --> 00:42:05.043
from the New York Times from 1939.

00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:09.270
Here's a national survey
that was fielded in 1938,

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:12.210
just when this national chain store tax

00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:13.380
was being considered

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:15.300
and people were asked,
well, what do you think

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:18.660
would be the best policy
toward the chain stores?

00:42:18.660 --> 00:42:20.310
And basically what you see here

00:42:20.310 --> 00:42:22.050
is that now most Americans thought

00:42:22.050 --> 00:42:23.430
that they should be left alone

00:42:23.430 --> 00:42:27.720
to "make what profits they can
rather than tax them extra."

00:42:27.720 --> 00:42:30.870
And certainly not to get
rid of them entirely,

00:42:30.870 --> 00:42:33.960
which had been Patman's objective there.

00:42:33.960 --> 00:42:38.640
So, again, the politics of
retailing in the 1920s and '30s

00:42:38.640 --> 00:42:42.540
in the US were really strikingly
different from Europe.

00:42:42.540 --> 00:42:45.300
Large retailers had already benefited

00:42:45.300 --> 00:42:47.610
from this enormous headstart

00:42:47.610 --> 00:42:51.060
by virtue of this ban on
resale price maintenance.

00:42:51.060 --> 00:42:54.630
And once they were operating
on a national scale,

00:42:54.630 --> 00:42:58.410
they were able to really work
around whatever state laws

00:42:58.410 --> 00:43:01.410
were getting it passed in this fractured,

00:43:01.410 --> 00:43:03.570
fragmented regulatory landscape.

00:43:03.570 --> 00:43:05.820
And again, as they grew,

00:43:05.820 --> 00:43:08.220
they were assembling
this ever expanding kind

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:11.820
of support coalition
that they were mobilizing

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:15.093
whenever they faced a
new regulatory threat.

00:43:16.590 --> 00:43:18.300
So finally, and very briefly

00:43:18.300 --> 00:43:20.190
to the post World War II period,

00:43:20.190 --> 00:43:21.390
this is of course the period

00:43:21.390 --> 00:43:23.460
that saw the kind of full flowering

00:43:23.460 --> 00:43:27.330
of America's consumption
driven growth model.

00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:29.310
This is the period that's really famous

00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:32.760
for the boom in consumption

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:37.140
largely as a result of
government-sponsored housing loans,

00:43:37.140 --> 00:43:39.870
at least for white middle class families.

00:43:39.870 --> 00:43:41.940
This is the period that saw the growth

00:43:41.940 --> 00:43:45.090
of these suburbs and these suburban malls

00:43:45.090 --> 00:43:47.670
that I was talking about at the beginning.

00:43:47.670 --> 00:43:51.360
But even as these new
suburban shopping centers

00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:55.110
were reconfiguring the
geography of shopping,

00:43:55.110 --> 00:43:59.760
you had disruptive new
entrance to the retail sector

00:43:59.760 --> 00:44:02.730
that would really redefine
the temporal bounds

00:44:02.730 --> 00:44:04.890
of shopping as well.

00:44:04.890 --> 00:44:06.780
So as I mentioned at the outset,

00:44:06.780 --> 00:44:08.700
despite some liberalization,

00:44:08.700 --> 00:44:11.460
really in general,
shopping hours in Europe

00:44:11.460 --> 00:44:13.680
are simply a lot more regulated

00:44:13.680 --> 00:44:15.540
than they are in the United States.

00:44:15.540 --> 00:44:18.120
You have shorter days,
but also in particular,

00:44:18.120 --> 00:44:22.620
you have a lot more
restrictions on Sunday shopping.

00:44:22.620 --> 00:44:24.810
And actually that was the
case in the US as well.

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:27.510
Sunday shopping was really not a thing

00:44:27.510 --> 00:44:30.510
in the immediate post World War II years.

00:44:30.510 --> 00:44:32.313
Shops were generally closed.

00:44:33.270 --> 00:44:36.660
But all of this started
falling apart in the 1960s

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:40.530
with the arrival of these very
disruptive new discounters.

00:44:40.530 --> 00:44:43.020
So you had a bunch of
kind of small operations

00:44:43.020 --> 00:44:45.060
that flamed out pretty quickly.

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:46.410
Those are the ones on the left,

00:44:46.410 --> 00:44:49.470
like, Korvettes was one of
the most famous of these.

00:44:49.470 --> 00:44:52.650
But this is also the period
that saw the founding

00:44:52.650 --> 00:44:55.077
of some of these bigger,
more durable chains.

00:44:55.077 --> 00:45:00.077
And so Kmart and Walmart and
Target, all three of those

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:04.200
were actually founded in
the same year, in 1962.

00:45:04.200 --> 00:45:06.510
And all of these discounters,

00:45:06.510 --> 00:45:09.120
they were challenging
established retailers,

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:10.440
not just on price,

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:13.620
though, of course, absolutely on price,

00:45:13.620 --> 00:45:16.890
but also simply by
staying open both evenings

00:45:16.890 --> 00:45:18.720
and also on Sundays,

00:45:18.720 --> 00:45:21.123
which at the time, again, was not a thing.

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:25.440
So, again, small retailers
and their allies mobilized

00:45:25.440 --> 00:45:28.020
to try to impose some restrictions here.

00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:31.020
They were joined by
religious groups often,

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:32.730
they were actually also joined

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:34.950
by some of the big department stores

00:45:34.950 --> 00:45:37.860
because department stores of
course had higher staffing

00:45:37.860 --> 00:45:40.080
and therefore higher labor costs

00:45:40.080 --> 00:45:43.980
and they weren't interested
in having the extended hours.

00:45:43.980 --> 00:45:47.130
But again, you get the same basic story.

00:45:47.130 --> 00:45:51.360
Again, you have a flurry of
state and local legislation

00:45:51.360 --> 00:45:54.690
that attempts to regulate Sunday selling.

00:45:54.690 --> 00:45:58.020
These laws took a variety of tax.

00:45:58.020 --> 00:46:00.240
Some of them tried to ban the sale

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:02.670
of items other than necessities.

00:46:02.670 --> 00:46:05.190
Some of them tried to limit Sunday selling

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:07.680
to particular kinds of retailers.

00:46:07.680 --> 00:46:10.920
Some of them tried to
prohibit only big stores

00:46:10.920 --> 00:46:14.310
from staying open on Sundays.

00:46:14.310 --> 00:46:16.620
But even where these laws were passed,

00:46:16.620 --> 00:46:20.850
these discounters often just
flagrantly ignored the rules

00:46:20.850 --> 00:46:22.680
and simply paid the fines.

00:46:22.680 --> 00:46:25.860
They were basically
absorbing these legal costs

00:46:25.860 --> 00:46:28.050
as just another business expense.

00:46:28.050 --> 00:46:30.450
It reminds me very much of the way

00:46:30.450 --> 00:46:33.630
that Uber entered local
transportation markets.

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:36.750
Basically, Uber just came in,

00:46:36.750 --> 00:46:40.290
in the US at least, ignored
local transportation laws

00:46:40.290 --> 00:46:43.623
and simply paid the fines
while they scaled up.

00:46:44.790 --> 00:46:48.720
So in Europe, this kind of rule breaking

00:46:48.720 --> 00:46:52.140
met with the opposition,
not just of small merchants,

00:46:52.140 --> 00:46:55.380
but also of religious groups and unions.

00:46:55.380 --> 00:46:58.710
But in the US, where
unions were much weaker,

00:46:58.710 --> 00:47:01.140
enforcement tended to
run through the courts.

00:47:01.140 --> 00:47:03.330
And when these things got litigated,

00:47:03.330 --> 00:47:05.580
retailers actually often won.

00:47:05.580 --> 00:47:07.740
And they were able to prevail often

00:47:07.740 --> 00:47:10.770
because they could claim a
discriminatory treatment,

00:47:10.770 --> 00:47:14.130
which met with a lot of
sympathy in US courts

00:47:14.130 --> 00:47:16.860
because they have often strong aversions

00:47:16.860 --> 00:47:18.690
to any kind of government intervention

00:47:18.690 --> 00:47:23.400
that might smack of
legislative favoritism.

00:47:23.400 --> 00:47:27.690
Most importantly though,
while these cases dragged on,

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:32.490
these stores just stayed open
and the people got used to it.

00:47:32.490 --> 00:47:36.060
And once they got used to
it, they wanted to keep it.

00:47:36.060 --> 00:47:39.750
And so here's a survey from the mid 1970s

00:47:39.750 --> 00:47:42.247
which asks, again, a
national survey asking,

00:47:42.247 --> 00:47:45.600
"how would you feel about
a law against stores

00:47:45.600 --> 00:47:47.010
being open on Sundays?"

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:48.090
And as you see here,

00:47:48.090 --> 00:47:51.780
a huge majority by this time opposed that.

00:47:51.780 --> 00:47:56.780
And even actually when state laws did say,

00:47:57.240 --> 00:47:59.850
yes, you may try to legislate

00:47:59.850 --> 00:48:02.880
or you may try to restrict Sunday hours,

00:48:02.880 --> 00:48:06.330
basically enforcement
was just a huge problem.

00:48:06.330 --> 00:48:10.350
You know, who were the
police supposed to arrest,

00:48:10.350 --> 00:48:11.880
shoppers, managers?

00:48:11.880 --> 00:48:14.610
It just wasn't clear.

00:48:14.610 --> 00:48:17.490
So all of this kind of
culminated in an important case

00:48:17.490 --> 00:48:19.800
before the New York Circuit,

00:48:19.800 --> 00:48:21.810
New York Court of Appeals

00:48:21.810 --> 00:48:24.360
that throughout a state
law that had been trying

00:48:24.360 --> 00:48:26.910
to limit Sunday shopping hours,

00:48:26.910 --> 00:48:30.120
and the court did so
based on the observation

00:48:30.120 --> 00:48:32.700
that those laws seemed to be incoherent,

00:48:32.700 --> 00:48:35.070
they seemed to be unenforceable.

00:48:35.070 --> 00:48:36.690
And this was dangerous

00:48:36.690 --> 00:48:39.480
because this encouraged
what the court considered

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:43.023
to be an erosive disrespect of the law.

00:48:44.070 --> 00:48:47.160
So over the next years, basically
what happened is that all

00:48:47.160 --> 00:48:50.610
of these states abandoned
their legal prohibitions,

00:48:50.610 --> 00:48:51.930
either explicitly

00:48:51.930 --> 00:48:55.710
or more often they just
stopped enforcing them.

00:48:55.710 --> 00:49:00.060
And this is how we ended
up with this uniquely,

00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:01.950
sort of compared to
other rich democracies,

00:49:01.950 --> 00:49:05.700
a uniquely unregulated
shopping hour regime

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:07.053
that we have today.

00:49:09.090 --> 00:49:13.350
So I wanna forego the
usual kind of conclusion.

00:49:13.350 --> 00:49:17.100
And so instead I want
to wrap up this talk,

00:49:17.100 --> 00:49:19.440
not by sort of repeating the argument

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:22.500
or by summarizing the
argument, but by stepping back

00:49:22.500 --> 00:49:24.360
and sort of panning out a little bit

00:49:24.360 --> 00:49:28.020
to highlight the significance
of these developments

00:49:28.020 --> 00:49:30.750
for the American political
economy as a whole.

00:49:30.750 --> 00:49:34.020
And I wanna also try to situate this study

00:49:34.020 --> 00:49:37.110
in the context of some of my previous work

00:49:37.110 --> 00:49:39.930
on inequality and particularly inequality

00:49:39.930 --> 00:49:41.880
at the low income,

00:49:41.880 --> 00:49:44.490
at the low end of the income ladder.

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:47.730
So one of the reasons that I
think it's really important

00:49:47.730 --> 00:49:49.470
to think about the evolution

00:49:49.470 --> 00:49:52.380
of discount retailing specifically,

00:49:52.380 --> 00:49:54.840
alongside the other sort of, I think,

00:49:54.840 --> 00:49:58.410
much better known story
of the role of credit

00:49:58.410 --> 00:50:01.530
within the post-war
American political economy

00:50:01.530 --> 00:50:04.320
is that it gives us a
somewhat different angle

00:50:04.320 --> 00:50:07.410
on the crisis of American manufacturing

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:12.093
and on rising inequality
in the US since the 1970s.

00:50:13.140 --> 00:50:14.700
So as I've tried to point out

00:50:14.700 --> 00:50:17.850
in some of my previous work
on labor and labor markets,

00:50:17.850 --> 00:50:21.480
one of the ways in which
the US really stands out

00:50:21.480 --> 00:50:23.310
in international comparisons

00:50:23.310 --> 00:50:27.087
is for the really large
size of the low pay sector.

00:50:27.087 --> 00:50:30.600
And so what you see on the
screen here are OECD figures

00:50:30.600 --> 00:50:32.223
on the share of low pay,

00:50:34.173 --> 00:50:36.390
the size of the low pay
sector as a percentage

00:50:36.390 --> 00:50:38.400
of the full workforce.

00:50:38.400 --> 00:50:42.810
Low pay here defined as
earning less than two thirds

00:50:42.810 --> 00:50:43.710
of median income.

00:50:43.710 --> 00:50:46.800
And what you see is that
the US is at the high end

00:50:46.800 --> 00:50:48.600
among its peers.

00:50:48.600 --> 00:50:51.780
The same story or much
the same story is true

00:50:51.780 --> 00:50:53.430
if you look at in-work poverty.

00:50:53.430 --> 00:50:55.230
I don't have the comparative figures,

00:50:55.230 --> 00:50:58.740
but I just looked at the most
recent figures for the US,

00:50:58.740 --> 00:51:03.540
which is from 2021, that
show that nearly 15%

00:51:03.540 --> 00:51:06.150
of Americans live in households

00:51:06.150 --> 00:51:10.290
with incomes below 60% of
median disposable income.

00:51:10.290 --> 00:51:14.850
And to put that in perspective,
the OECD average is 9.3%.

00:51:14.850 --> 00:51:18.420
So, again, very much at the high end.

00:51:18.420 --> 00:51:20.760
Beyond that, and again,
as I've tried to point out

00:51:20.760 --> 00:51:22.860
in some of my other work,

00:51:22.860 --> 00:51:26.130
these low paid jobs in the
US really just don't come

00:51:26.130 --> 00:51:27.420
with a lot of the benefits

00:51:27.420 --> 00:51:30.000
that are taken for
granted in Europe, right?

00:51:30.000 --> 00:51:31.170
Healthcare benefits,

00:51:31.170 --> 00:51:32.280
there is, in this country,

00:51:32.280 --> 00:51:34.830
no federally guaranteed paid time off.

00:51:34.830 --> 00:51:35.910
There is, in this country,

00:51:35.910 --> 00:51:38.160
no federally guaranteed vacation days.

00:51:38.160 --> 00:51:40.560
There's no rules regarding scheduling.

00:51:40.560 --> 00:51:44.610
And so precarious workers in
particular are often subject

00:51:44.610 --> 00:51:49.140
to extraordinarily irregular,
unstable work hours.

00:51:49.140 --> 00:51:51.480
These are all ways in which the US

00:51:51.480 --> 00:51:56.480
is an enormous outlier in
international perspective.

00:51:56.820 --> 00:52:00.570
And these types of precarious
working relationships

00:52:00.570 --> 00:52:04.110
have really grown
especially since the 1970s.

00:52:04.110 --> 00:52:07.440
So a common, you know, not
unreasonable explanation

00:52:07.440 --> 00:52:11.760
for that is the decline
of American manufacturing

00:52:11.760 --> 00:52:12.780
in the face of, you know,

00:52:12.780 --> 00:52:16.050
competition from lower
cost producers abroad.

00:52:16.050 --> 00:52:17.640
And that's of course what's seen

00:52:17.640 --> 00:52:19.530
as having destroyed all these kind

00:52:19.530 --> 00:52:23.280
of better paid unionized
manufacturing jobs.

00:52:23.280 --> 00:52:27.798
And that's what left us
with a lot of low skill,

00:52:27.798 --> 00:52:30.453
low pay service sector jobs.

00:52:32.100 --> 00:52:35.040
That account is of course not wrong,

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:37.380
but I think it's radically incomplete

00:52:37.380 --> 00:52:39.390
because in that model,

00:52:39.390 --> 00:52:42.060
the pressures on American manufacturing

00:52:42.060 --> 00:52:43.980
are entirely exogenous.

00:52:43.980 --> 00:52:46.020
You know, American retailers are not seen

00:52:46.020 --> 00:52:48.810
as having played any particular role here.

00:52:48.810 --> 00:52:49.800
They're just seen as some kind

00:52:49.800 --> 00:52:52.050
of a passive transmission belt.

00:52:52.050 --> 00:52:54.930
These stores are just
offering consumers a choice

00:52:54.930 --> 00:52:58.890
and consumers are going
for the lower prices.

00:52:58.890 --> 00:53:01.380
But in the story that I'm trying to tell,

00:53:01.380 --> 00:53:04.140
these large low cost retailers

00:53:04.140 --> 00:53:07.110
are really playing a much more active role

00:53:07.110 --> 00:53:10.950
in accelerating the decline
of American manufacturing

00:53:10.950 --> 00:53:14.460
and undermining the American
growth model more generally.

00:53:14.460 --> 00:53:16.530
I think it's really important

00:53:16.530 --> 00:53:19.140
that the factors that I'm
trying to highlight here,

00:53:19.140 --> 00:53:23.100
this legal regime and the
fractured regulatory landscape,

00:53:23.100 --> 00:53:27.960
that promoted a particular
model of retailing to dominance.

00:53:27.960 --> 00:53:30.420
It's an ultra lean retailing model.

00:53:30.420 --> 00:53:32.910
It's based on extremely narrow margins,

00:53:32.910 --> 00:53:37.020
is dominated by big players,
Walmart, now Amazon,

00:53:37.020 --> 00:53:39.210
that enjoy enormous power,

00:53:39.210 --> 00:53:42.870
both over workers and
over their suppliers.

00:53:42.870 --> 00:53:44.370
So of course these companies,

00:53:44.370 --> 00:53:45.540
one of the things that they're doing

00:53:45.540 --> 00:53:48.630
is that they're cutting their
own labor costs to the bone.

00:53:48.630 --> 00:53:50.910
And as you see here,
that's a very old recipe.

00:53:50.910 --> 00:53:53.880
That goes all the way back
to, you know, Frank Woolworth,

00:53:53.880 --> 00:53:58.260
who in 1892 already was
saying, we must have cheap help

00:53:58.260 --> 00:54:01.080
or we can't sell cheap goods.

00:54:01.080 --> 00:54:03.480
And as you see, I put some figures here,

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:05.850
as you see, retail workers

00:54:05.850 --> 00:54:09.600
in the US themselves make
up a very significant share

00:54:09.600 --> 00:54:12.360
of this American precarious
that I was talking about.

00:54:12.360 --> 00:54:15.960
Over three quarters of them
fall in that low wage category

00:54:15.960 --> 00:54:17.610
that I just showed you.

00:54:17.610 --> 00:54:22.170
Fully 15% of them qualify for Medicaid.

00:54:22.170 --> 00:54:23.700
According to a recent study,

00:54:23.700 --> 00:54:26.880
2022 study from the Government
Accountability Office,

00:54:26.880 --> 00:54:29.100
Amazon and especially Walmart

00:54:29.100 --> 00:54:31.080
are among the top employers

00:54:31.080 --> 00:54:33.720
whose workers depend on SNAP benefits,

00:54:33.720 --> 00:54:36.153
what we used to call food stamps.

00:54:37.410 --> 00:54:39.600
Beyond their own labor model though,

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:41.070
these dominant discounters

00:54:41.070 --> 00:54:43.710
have also been extraordinarily aggressive,

00:54:43.710 --> 00:54:47.460
enforcing the manufacturers
and the vendors who supply them

00:54:47.460 --> 00:54:49.740
to accept ever narrower margins

00:54:49.740 --> 00:54:52.260
and therefore to cut their own costs.

00:54:52.260 --> 00:54:55.680
So Walmart is of course famous for its,

00:54:55.680 --> 00:54:57.030
you know, using its dominance

00:54:57.030 --> 00:55:01.200
to extract extremely low
prices from its suppliers,

00:55:01.200 --> 00:55:05.190
many of whom rely really very heavily

00:55:05.190 --> 00:55:08.610
on this single company for a huge share

00:55:08.610 --> 00:55:10.830
of their total sales.

00:55:10.830 --> 00:55:12.767
And these are pressures,
of course, that, you know,

00:55:12.767 --> 00:55:17.073
in the 1970s, these producers
began passing on to workers,

00:55:18.510 --> 00:55:21.000
you know, first by moving
to the non-union South

00:55:21.000 --> 00:55:24.810
and later by outsourcing or subcontracting

00:55:24.810 --> 00:55:27.750
with lower cost producers abroad.

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:30.480
And similarly today, of course Amazon

00:55:30.480 --> 00:55:33.660
is able to extract really high fees

00:55:33.660 --> 00:55:35.850
from many of the third party sellers

00:55:35.850 --> 00:55:38.880
who are literally existentially reliant

00:55:38.880 --> 00:55:42.510
on this platform in order
to reach their customers.

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:45.900
And what makes these
companies hard to regulate,

00:55:45.900 --> 00:55:49.710
especially in the US, is
that just as in the past,

00:55:49.710 --> 00:55:53.550
all the while they're actively
cultivating this large,

00:55:53.550 --> 00:55:55.650
loyal customer base.

00:55:55.650 --> 00:56:00.650
So prime membership globally
is now up to about 200 million,

00:56:01.740 --> 00:56:05.010
more than three quarters
of them are Americans.

00:56:05.010 --> 00:56:08.100
That may be not so surprising,
it's an American company,

00:56:08.100 --> 00:56:11.220
but what did surprise me
is that fully two-thirds

00:56:11.220 --> 00:56:14.340
of all online shoppers
in the United States,

00:56:14.340 --> 00:56:18.690
and that's a lot of people,
are Amazon Prime members.

00:56:18.690 --> 00:56:22.380
So you begin to get a
kind of normalization

00:56:22.380 --> 00:56:25.080
of these relationships
and growing popularity

00:56:25.080 --> 00:56:28.200
as more and more people
come to kind of depend

00:56:28.200 --> 00:56:30.870
on these services and start to think

00:56:30.870 --> 00:56:34.500
that it's perfectly normal to
expect these low priced goods

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:36.040
to be delivered to their door

00:56:37.439 --> 00:56:39.450
by many of these precarious workers

00:56:39.450 --> 00:56:40.800
that I was just talking about,

00:56:40.800 --> 00:56:43.950
delivered within, used to
be 48 hours, now is 24,

00:56:43.950 --> 00:56:48.210
increasingly it's, in some
places, same day service.

00:56:48.210 --> 00:56:53.070
And then once you're in this
cycle of, you know, low margins

00:56:53.070 --> 00:56:57.330
and low wages, you also then
begin to have this population

00:56:57.330 --> 00:57:01.500
of low income workers who rely
on these low cost retailers

00:57:01.500 --> 00:57:04.050
to make ends meet, right?

00:57:04.050 --> 00:57:07.950
So in this context, these
large discount retailers

00:57:07.950 --> 00:57:11.940
have paradoxically kind of
become part of the infrastructure

00:57:11.940 --> 00:57:13.950
of the lives of many citizens

00:57:13.950 --> 00:57:18.300
and not just for the low
prices that they offer, right?

00:57:18.300 --> 00:57:19.740
For people like me

00:57:19.740 --> 00:57:21.720
or probably most of the
people in this room,

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:25.320
who have, you know, pretty
good pay, very flexible jobs,

00:57:25.320 --> 00:57:28.710
you know, we may prefer to
shop at our local stores,

00:57:28.710 --> 00:57:31.650
that's my local bookstore
on the left there,

00:57:31.650 --> 00:57:33.030
but for people with low incomes,

00:57:33.030 --> 00:57:36.960
people who work really long
hours, sometimes two jobs,

00:57:36.960 --> 00:57:39.810
people who have no paid
time off, no vacation days,

00:57:39.810 --> 00:57:44.460
and certainly can't pay
the high cost of daycare,

00:57:44.460 --> 00:57:46.080
for people like that,

00:57:46.080 --> 00:57:49.413
and that's a really
large share of Americans,

00:57:50.280 --> 00:57:53.100
it's not an option
necessarily to shop around

00:57:53.100 --> 00:57:55.710
at a lot of smaller specialty stores.

00:57:55.710 --> 00:57:59.040
It may seem more realistic just
to go to some big box store

00:57:59.040 --> 00:58:01.020
at whatever time of night and day

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:02.340
and buy everything you need

00:58:02.340 --> 00:58:04.923
at this low price kind of all in one go.

00:58:06.300 --> 00:58:09.420
Finally, it's not just consumers

00:58:09.420 --> 00:58:12.570
who have come to rely
on these big retailers.

00:58:12.570 --> 00:58:15.870
So when the pandemic hit, you know,

00:58:15.870 --> 00:58:19.920
European countries mobilized
their healthcare infrastructure

00:58:19.920 --> 00:58:23.700
in order to administer vaccines, right?

00:58:23.700 --> 00:58:27.300
In the US what happened, in
the US the government turned

00:58:27.300 --> 00:58:31.260
to these big retailers,
CVS, Walgreens, and Walmart,

00:58:31.260 --> 00:58:34.950
as kind of a key part of
the essential infrastructure

00:58:34.950 --> 00:58:37.020
to vaccinate the public.

00:58:37.020 --> 00:58:40.020
So Walmart played a really central role

00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:41.640
in reaching rural communities

00:58:41.640 --> 00:58:43.920
that are otherwise completely underserved

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:46.080
by the American healthcare system.

00:58:46.080 --> 00:58:49.020
They played a big role in
reaching low income groups

00:58:49.020 --> 00:58:51.960
that, again, lack the paid
time off that you might need

00:58:51.960 --> 00:58:54.030
to visit a clinic during the day.

00:58:54.030 --> 00:58:57.420
You could go to Walmart at
10:00 PM and get your shot.

00:58:57.420 --> 00:59:02.010
These are things that Americans
kind of take for granted

00:59:02.010 --> 00:59:04.230
or don't think that much about,

00:59:04.230 --> 00:59:07.830
but they are highly
anomalous to Europeans.

00:59:07.830 --> 00:59:11.160
I mean, my European friends
were shocked to hear

00:59:11.160 --> 00:59:14.670
that I got one of my
shots at a Walmart, right?

00:59:14.670 --> 00:59:18.660
So my point is absolutely not

00:59:18.660 --> 00:59:21.270
to celebrate these big discounters.

00:59:21.270 --> 00:59:23.770
I hope I've made eminently clear

00:59:24.810 --> 00:59:28.200
that I'm a critic particularly
of their labor model.

00:59:28.200 --> 00:59:29.670
I just wanna point out

00:59:29.670 --> 00:59:33.750
that the US is in a very
deep equilibrium here.

00:59:33.750 --> 00:59:37.650
One that is I think sustained
not just by the popularity

00:59:37.650 --> 00:59:40.560
of these big retailers, though that too,

00:59:40.560 --> 00:59:43.470
but also really by the
infrastructural role

00:59:43.470 --> 00:59:47.670
that they have come to play
in the lives of many citizens.

00:59:47.670 --> 00:59:48.660
So thank you very much.

00:59:48.660 --> 00:59:50.045
I'm looking forward to your question.

00:59:50.045 --> 00:59:53.045
(audience applauds)

00:59:57.307 --> 00:59:58.620
- Thank you so much.

00:59:58.620 --> 01:00:01.598
So we have plenty of time for Q&amp;A.

01:00:01.598 --> 01:00:03.181
We have until 5:30.

01:00:04.160 --> 01:00:06.391
So let's open the floor for questions

01:00:06.391 --> 01:00:08.240
and maybe we can give a chance

01:00:08.240 --> 01:00:11.703
to students first couple of questions.

01:00:14.185 --> 01:00:19.185
Any students from EP&amp;A or
political science (indistinct)?

01:00:21.074 --> 01:00:21.907
Hi, Amy.

01:00:23.520 --> 01:00:25.050
- Like, you kind of
mentioned near the end there,

01:00:25.050 --> 01:00:26.790
there's that paradoxical relationship

01:00:26.790 --> 01:00:31.110
that exists where a lot of,
like, lower income people

01:00:31.110 --> 01:00:35.550
are simultaneously hurt by
the big retailers that exist,

01:00:35.550 --> 01:00:38.880
but also sort of depend on 'em
in order to get basic goods

01:00:38.880 --> 01:00:41.010
and necessities and things like that.

01:00:41.010 --> 01:00:45.520
What do you think the
most, like, actionable way

01:00:46.505 --> 01:00:49.023
like, is to move forward and, you know,

01:00:50.490 --> 01:00:52.110
like, be able to solve the fact

01:00:52.110 --> 01:00:55.650
that these low income people
stop getting corrupted

01:00:55.650 --> 01:00:58.590
by these large retailers,
but also still have access

01:00:58.590 --> 01:01:01.560
to basic necessities and things like that?

01:01:01.560 --> 01:01:03.150
- Okay, great, thank you.

01:01:03.150 --> 01:01:04.440
It's a great question.

01:01:04.440 --> 01:01:09.440
So policy, I mean, my whole
message is that policy

01:01:11.700 --> 01:01:14.730
and politics got us into
this deep equilibrium,

01:01:14.730 --> 01:01:17.760
and I believe that policy and politics

01:01:17.760 --> 01:01:22.170
are the way out of this bind as well.

01:01:22.170 --> 01:01:24.660
I don't have a ton of faith

01:01:24.660 --> 01:01:29.130
in solutions like kind of
collective self-restraint

01:01:29.130 --> 01:01:31.110
on the part of consumers.

01:01:31.110 --> 01:01:32.880
I think sort of everything,

01:01:32.880 --> 01:01:35.553
all the work that I've
ever done on platforms,

01:01:36.570 --> 01:01:39.270
this and other kinds of platforms,

01:01:39.270 --> 01:01:41.490
doesn't give me a whole
lot of faith that just,

01:01:41.490 --> 01:01:43.710
you know, sort of asking
consumers to sort of like,

01:01:43.710 --> 01:01:46.830
don't do it, isn't gonna get us very far.

01:01:46.830 --> 01:01:50.103
And so, you know, my
friend, Jenny Mansbridge,

01:01:51.300 --> 01:01:54.630
has a theory of, you
know, legitimate coercion.

01:01:54.630 --> 01:01:59.630
The role of government is to pass policies

01:02:00.330 --> 01:02:03.453
that involve legitimate
coercion sort to force,

01:02:05.070 --> 01:02:08.190
to use policy to force people to do things

01:02:08.190 --> 01:02:09.930
that they wouldn't otherwise do,

01:02:09.930 --> 01:02:12.000
and to place, you know,

01:02:12.000 --> 01:02:14.730
sort of solve societal
collective action problems

01:02:14.730 --> 01:02:15.600
through policy.

01:02:15.600 --> 01:02:19.500
And so the two that I
would point to would be,

01:02:19.500 --> 01:02:23.970
first of all, I think
antitrust legislation

01:02:23.970 --> 01:02:24.810
is really important.

01:02:24.810 --> 01:02:27.600
And I think that, I mean, I
basically agree with Lina Khan,

01:02:27.600 --> 01:02:30.120
who's currently the head of FTC,

01:02:30.120 --> 01:02:31.350
that these are structural problems.

01:02:31.350 --> 01:02:33.300
They require structural solutions.

01:02:33.300 --> 01:02:38.040
I think the EU has shown
the way in some areas

01:02:38.040 --> 01:02:42.240
of how to restrain some
of these big sellers

01:02:42.240 --> 01:02:45.780
in terms of, like, limits
on predatory pricing,

01:02:45.780 --> 01:02:49.380
limits on discriminatory
treatment and things like that.

01:02:49.380 --> 01:02:51.870
It's still in development,

01:02:51.870 --> 01:02:54.030
but I think there are models there.

01:02:54.030 --> 01:02:55.560
And then, you know, I'm a labor scholar.

01:02:55.560 --> 01:03:00.560
And so labor law reform
is absolutely essential.

01:03:01.110 --> 01:03:04.466
So one of the things that
I try to do in the book is,

01:03:04.466 --> 01:03:06.510
and I didn't have time to do in the talk,

01:03:06.510 --> 01:03:09.150
is I compare the labor,
you know, the situation

01:03:09.150 --> 01:03:14.150
in the retail market in European
countries and in the US.

01:03:14.160 --> 01:03:16.680
And you know, in all of these countries,

01:03:16.680 --> 01:03:19.590
you know, retail workers
are not very well organized,

01:03:19.590 --> 01:03:21.840
but in Europe, they're far better covered

01:03:21.840 --> 01:03:25.230
by collective contracts that,
you know, regulate wages,

01:03:25.230 --> 01:03:28.980
that regulate working hours, that provide,

01:03:28.980 --> 01:03:32.880
you know, some of these
core foundational rights

01:03:32.880 --> 01:03:36.003
that in this country are seen as, like,

01:03:37.410 --> 01:03:40.140
fringe benefits or
something that you might get

01:03:40.140 --> 01:03:45.140
if your employer is sort
of particularly generous

01:03:45.210 --> 01:03:47.040
or something like that.

01:03:47.040 --> 01:03:49.440
So, yeah, those would be the two,

01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:51.300
and especially labor law reform,

01:03:51.300 --> 01:03:53.160
I just think that it's
absolutely critical.

01:03:53.160 --> 01:03:54.570
And again, we have, you know,

01:03:54.570 --> 01:03:58.050
it's a moment of labor activism

01:03:58.050 --> 01:04:00.340
and so I have actually quite a lot of hope

01:04:01.394 --> 01:04:03.844
that things could move in
that direction as well.

01:04:09.630 --> 01:04:11.531
- [Man] So returning to the point you made

01:04:11.531 --> 01:04:14.610
about political mobilization

01:04:14.610 --> 01:04:17.130
and the ability of these retailers

01:04:17.130 --> 01:04:20.340
to kind of leverage
their essential services

01:04:20.340 --> 01:04:23.177
to, you know, generate goodwill
that they can call upon,

01:04:23.177 --> 01:04:26.250
you know, in moments of, you
know, potential regulation.

01:04:26.250 --> 01:04:31.250
I'm wondering if you
think that contemporarily,

01:04:31.500 --> 01:04:33.807
we're kind of running up
against the limits of that

01:04:33.807 --> 01:04:37.440
and that it seems like the
capacity of corporations

01:04:37.440 --> 01:04:42.090
to kind of, in that way
leverage public support

01:04:42.090 --> 01:04:44.170
against regulations seem reduced

01:04:45.060 --> 01:04:46.590
and that kind of challenges

01:04:46.590 --> 01:04:49.350
to vote in terms of,
like, racial antitrust,

01:04:49.350 --> 01:04:53.013
but also in terms of, you
know, legal organizing.

01:04:55.080 --> 01:04:55.913
They seem,

01:04:57.048 --> 01:05:00.960
and this is anecdotal but,
like, increasingly successful

01:05:00.960 --> 01:05:03.723
and kind of challenging
them despite the fact that,

01:05:04.590 --> 01:05:07.140
you know, you have a
corporation like Amazon

01:05:07.140 --> 01:05:09.780
that kind of makes it
explicit that business model

01:05:09.780 --> 01:05:14.160
is dependent on low-wage labor.

01:05:14.160 --> 01:05:15.930
And so I'm wondering

01:05:15.930 --> 01:05:18.900
about, like, the
contemporary possibilities

01:05:18.900 --> 01:05:23.900
for challenging retailers
despite this history

01:05:24.930 --> 01:05:26.790
of kind of goodwill

01:05:26.790 --> 01:05:31.248
and this sense of kind of
belovedness among population

01:05:31.248 --> 01:05:32.523
- Right.

01:05:34.800 --> 01:05:36.660
One of the things that's distinctive

01:05:36.660 --> 01:05:39.420
and that's different about
Europe versus the United States,

01:05:39.420 --> 01:05:40.593
thanks for coming, Amy.

01:05:41.520 --> 01:05:42.690
One of the things that's different

01:05:42.690 --> 01:05:47.070
is that there are very few
sort of countervailing forces,

01:05:47.070 --> 01:05:49.200
organized interests countervailing

01:05:49.200 --> 01:05:51.180
that are capable of
countervailing the power

01:05:51.180 --> 01:05:54.510
of these monopolists or
would-be monopolists, right?

01:05:54.510 --> 01:05:57.930
So it's like they can develop
this direct relationship

01:05:57.930 --> 01:06:00.240
to consumers because
there are very few sort

01:06:00.240 --> 01:06:02.370
of intermediary organizations

01:06:02.370 --> 01:06:06.300
that can sort of perform
countervailing functions.

01:06:06.300 --> 01:06:09.300
And so, you know, unions would be one,

01:06:09.300 --> 01:06:13.830
these are associations that
have actually a presence

01:06:13.830 --> 01:06:17.930
and that have performed
countervailing functions in Europe,

01:06:19.740 --> 01:06:21.390
but that are sort of absent

01:06:21.390 --> 01:06:22.797
or extremely weak in the United States.

01:06:22.797 --> 01:06:25.020
And so I've already talked about labor.

01:06:25.020 --> 01:06:28.290
The other one, I would say, is
actually trade associations,

01:06:28.290 --> 01:06:30.210
organized employers,

01:06:30.210 --> 01:06:32.520
because in a lot of the
work that I've done on,

01:06:32.520 --> 01:06:34.110
again, on these platforms,

01:06:34.110 --> 01:06:36.240
especially platforms that kind of move in

01:06:36.240 --> 01:06:40.020
and come to dominate
and often are doing so

01:06:40.020 --> 01:06:42.060
by working in sort of gray areas

01:06:42.060 --> 01:06:44.940
or sort of even illegally to begin

01:06:44.940 --> 01:06:48.060
and just kind of paying these
fines and stuff like that.

01:06:48.060 --> 01:06:51.030
One of the most important
countervailing forces

01:06:51.030 --> 01:06:54.060
in many European countries is
organized employer interests,

01:06:54.060 --> 01:06:57.510
because organized employers
also don't have a big interest

01:06:57.510 --> 01:07:02.510
in some firm gaining a
foothold in the market.

01:07:03.060 --> 01:07:06.450
And they would much prefer
sort of, especially those

01:07:06.450 --> 01:07:09.720
who have sort of monopolistic
ambitions, right?

01:07:09.720 --> 01:07:14.550
So they are often defenders
of a well-regulated market.

01:07:14.550 --> 01:07:16.173
One that certainly sort of,

01:07:17.580 --> 01:07:21.000
certainly puts up some barriers
to the kinds of illegal

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:25.500
or sort of semi-legal
tactics that are often used

01:07:25.500 --> 01:07:28.650
in the early phases in
order to gain scale.

01:07:28.650 --> 01:07:32.460
So this is, again, one of
the, I think, casualties

01:07:32.460 --> 01:07:35.380
of American antitrust

01:07:36.270 --> 01:07:38.520
because, you know, going all the way back

01:07:38.520 --> 01:07:41.550
to the late 19th century,

01:07:41.550 --> 01:07:46.550
what we know is that the American,

01:07:46.680 --> 01:07:48.420
I mean, the story that
everybody kind of, I think,

01:07:48.420 --> 01:07:51.357
knows is that antitrust
was used against unions.

01:07:51.357 --> 01:07:53.640
And one of the reasons
that unions are so weak

01:07:53.640 --> 01:07:58.530
is because unions organized
on a more industrial level

01:07:58.530 --> 01:08:00.600
were sort of disarticulated partly

01:08:00.600 --> 01:08:04.560
because they were constantly
facing conspiracy charges

01:08:04.560 --> 01:08:05.700
and so forth.

01:08:05.700 --> 01:08:08.520
Another big casualty of that period

01:08:08.520 --> 01:08:12.270
that people don't talk as much
about is trade associations.

01:08:12.270 --> 01:08:15.870
Trade associations were very
much enfeebled in this period

01:08:15.870 --> 01:08:18.300
because the courts in the United States,

01:08:18.300 --> 01:08:20.280
again, distinctively

01:08:20.280 --> 01:08:22.770
and very unlike what
was going on in Europe,

01:08:22.770 --> 01:08:26.910
courts in the United States
were extremely averse,

01:08:26.910 --> 01:08:30.360
were extremely negative toward any form

01:08:30.360 --> 01:08:34.290
of horizontal coordination
among small competitors.

01:08:34.290 --> 01:08:37.350
It's to do with the
period that I talked about

01:08:37.350 --> 01:08:38.520
when Brandeis was saying,

01:08:38.520 --> 01:08:41.070
well, shouldn't we let
these small competitors,

01:08:41.070 --> 01:08:44.160
so to coordinate their
activities in order to,

01:08:44.160 --> 01:08:46.020
you know, be able to hold their own

01:08:46.020 --> 01:08:48.000
against these dominant players.

01:08:48.000 --> 01:08:50.100
And he was actually an advocate

01:08:50.100 --> 01:08:52.470
of something like would've been,

01:08:52.470 --> 01:08:54.510
you know, stronger trade associations.

01:08:54.510 --> 01:08:56.733
And they really atrophied in this country.

01:08:57.600 --> 01:09:00.600
And they atrophied because while antitrust

01:09:00.600 --> 01:09:04.020
was very forbearing toward large,

01:09:04.020 --> 01:09:06.360
you know, conglomerate kinds of companies,

01:09:06.360 --> 01:09:09.120
because with everything organized,

01:09:09.120 --> 01:09:11.730
all the functions organized
within a single entity,

01:09:11.730 --> 01:09:14.040
there's no collusion,
there's no conspiracy,

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:15.630
but as soon as there was any kind

01:09:15.630 --> 01:09:19.320
of horizontal coordination,
whether by unions,

01:09:19.320 --> 01:09:23.310
whether by workers, or by small retailers

01:09:23.310 --> 01:09:25.290
or small businesses,

01:09:25.290 --> 01:09:28.830
that actually ran afoul
of American antitrust.

01:09:28.830 --> 01:09:32.550
And so, you know, fast
forward to the present,

01:09:32.550 --> 01:09:35.620
those are exactly the kinds
of countervailing forces

01:09:37.242 --> 01:09:39.960
that are actually more
effective, I would say,

01:09:39.960 --> 01:09:42.420
politically than sort of organized

01:09:42.420 --> 01:09:44.370
or sort of collective self-restraint

01:09:44.370 --> 01:09:48.840
on the part of consumers who have, like,

01:09:48.840 --> 01:09:52.230
you know, for whom sort of
collective action problems

01:09:52.230 --> 01:09:54.243
are just, like, insurmountable.

01:09:56.820 --> 01:10:00.173
Oh, should I do something?

01:10:01.703 --> 01:10:04.860
- I had a question about what you think,

01:10:04.860 --> 01:10:07.890
what effect technology's gonna have, like,

01:10:07.890 --> 01:10:11.220
specifically on, I'm thinking
about just, like, trends

01:10:11.220 --> 01:10:13.820
in e-commerce and how expensive it is

01:10:13.820 --> 01:10:16.440
to have the digital infrastructure
and logistics for that,

01:10:16.440 --> 01:10:19.320
which obviously will
make it harder to compete

01:10:19.320 --> 01:10:23.550
for smaller retailers,
but also in terms of labor

01:10:23.550 --> 01:10:27.480
and potentially being
able to cut costs on that,

01:10:27.480 --> 01:10:30.660
like, using AI or other
forms of technology

01:10:30.660 --> 01:10:32.220
and whether you think that's something

01:10:32.220 --> 01:10:34.263
that can be regulated.

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:38.910
- Well, yeah, I mean the data

01:10:38.910 --> 01:10:42.810
is really crucial to these big platforms,

01:10:42.810 --> 01:10:44.670
you know, or these big
retailers generally,

01:10:44.670 --> 01:10:46.230
sort of Walmart as well.

01:10:46.230 --> 01:10:48.033
And it does two things, like,

01:10:50.610 --> 01:10:52.197
there's always been a
kind of balance of power

01:10:52.197 --> 01:10:53.760
and a kind of tension.

01:10:53.760 --> 01:10:57.390
You'd think that mass retail
and mass manufacturing

01:10:57.390 --> 01:10:58.440
would kind of go together.

01:10:58.440 --> 01:11:00.720
They'd be sort of symbiotically
related to one another,

01:11:00.720 --> 01:11:02.160
but in fact at many points,

01:11:02.160 --> 01:11:04.050
they've really been at
loggerheads with one another

01:11:04.050 --> 01:11:06.180
over things like pricing.

01:11:06.180 --> 01:11:08.970
And so the balance of power

01:11:08.970 --> 01:11:12.807
has shifted very much in
favor of large retailers,

01:11:14.340 --> 01:11:16.470
who now have the data

01:11:16.470 --> 01:11:19.320
to be able to dictate to their suppliers.

01:11:19.320 --> 01:11:21.480
You know, they know when,

01:11:21.480 --> 01:11:23.910
you know, what is selling at what price,

01:11:23.910 --> 01:11:27.120
in combination with what other
things, in what locations.

01:11:27.120 --> 01:11:29.400
And so they're able to really use that

01:11:29.400 --> 01:11:34.180
to dominate the suppliers and vendors

01:11:35.753 --> 01:11:37.203
that they get the goods from.

01:11:38.070 --> 01:11:41.550
So the use of technology,

01:11:41.550 --> 01:11:46.180
that's something that has
really bounded to the benefit

01:11:48.650 --> 01:11:50.070
of these big retailers.

01:11:50.070 --> 01:11:51.570
And the same thing with labor.

01:11:51.570 --> 01:11:54.030
Brishen Rogers has just
written a wonderful book

01:11:54.030 --> 01:11:56.940
on the way in which these big companies,

01:11:56.940 --> 01:12:00.090
not just, you know, Walmart and Amazon,

01:12:00.090 --> 01:12:03.420
although those two also,

01:12:03.420 --> 01:12:06.657
is used to surveil workers,
is used to control workers,

01:12:06.657 --> 01:12:10.770
and is used to sort of speed up, you know,

01:12:10.770 --> 01:12:15.360
sort of accelerate work in
various ways in that, you know,

01:12:15.360 --> 01:12:19.140
that is coded in the United
States under current antitrust

01:12:19.140 --> 01:12:22.140
or at least Chicago school
antitrust thinking as efficient,

01:12:22.140 --> 01:12:26.190
but that is in fact
extraordinarily exploitative.

01:12:26.190 --> 01:12:29.790
And so, so far I would say that the data

01:12:29.790 --> 01:12:33.570
that the sort of the
technology has operated

01:12:33.570 --> 01:12:38.570
to tilt the balance of power
further in favor of retailers,

01:12:38.760 --> 01:12:43.590
both vis-a-vis manufacturers
and vis-a-vis workers.

01:12:43.590 --> 01:12:44.760
Can you do something about that?

01:12:44.760 --> 01:12:47.550
Yeah, and again, I mean,
I hate to keep saying,

01:12:47.550 --> 01:12:50.340
but the EU is actually doing
some stuff in that regard

01:12:50.340 --> 01:12:54.600
that protects, that sort
of places some limits

01:12:54.600 --> 01:12:57.270
on the extent to which these big retailers

01:12:57.270 --> 01:13:01.890
can use their access to data in order to,

01:13:01.890 --> 01:13:05.640
I don't know, cannibalize,
like, for Amazon,

01:13:05.640 --> 01:13:07.980
there's a rule that limits their ability

01:13:07.980 --> 01:13:11.250
to cannibalize their third party sellers.

01:13:11.250 --> 01:13:15.060
Like, Amazon has this strategy
where if they see something,

01:13:15.060 --> 01:13:16.380
because they have the data,

01:13:16.380 --> 01:13:18.420
if they see something is
selling particularly well,

01:13:18.420 --> 01:13:21.570
they'll jump in and do
an Amazon basic knockoff

01:13:21.570 --> 01:13:22.403
of that product.

01:13:22.403 --> 01:13:23.940
And so there's some limits on that.

01:13:23.940 --> 01:13:27.450
And similarly, one of the things
that's pretty foundational

01:13:27.450 --> 01:13:30.030
to labor law in most of Europe

01:13:30.030 --> 01:13:35.030
is limitations on the use
of data to surveil workers.

01:13:35.040 --> 01:13:36.930
You can certainly can't surveil them

01:13:36.930 --> 01:13:38.640
when it comes to organizing activities

01:13:38.640 --> 01:13:40.500
and you also can't surveil them

01:13:40.500 --> 01:13:43.380
in terms of their sort of
the everyday pace of work.

01:13:43.380 --> 01:13:45.930
So, again, I think so far it hasn't worked

01:13:45.930 --> 01:13:47.610
that way in the US, but that's, again,

01:13:47.610 --> 01:13:51.450
policy, politics could
re-equilibrate things

01:13:51.450 --> 01:13:52.550
at least a little bit.

01:13:55.650 --> 01:13:58.087
- [Woman] But then, I
think you can (indistinct)

01:13:58.087 --> 01:14:00.000
- [Woman] Hi, thank you so much.

01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:01.620
Premise, I'm from Europe

01:14:01.620 --> 01:14:04.020
and perhaps because of
that I'm not so positive

01:14:04.020 --> 01:14:06.540
about the part of the
world that I come from.

01:14:06.540 --> 01:14:09.960
And I'm wondering whether
you think that Europe

01:14:09.960 --> 01:14:13.440
will be going in the
same direction of the US

01:14:13.440 --> 01:14:14.760
because while,

01:14:14.760 --> 01:14:19.110
like, we obviously have a
very strong welfare state

01:14:19.110 --> 01:14:21.720
in certain countries
of the European Union.

01:14:21.720 --> 01:14:24.090
In particular in the south,

01:14:24.090 --> 01:14:26.220
we are so severely underpaid

01:14:26.220 --> 01:14:31.220
that we necessarily have
to rely on big retailers.

01:14:31.230 --> 01:14:33.420
So if you gave the same speech,

01:14:33.420 --> 01:14:35.640
I work at the European
University Institute,

01:14:35.640 --> 01:14:38.963
most people, most PhD
will be amongst those

01:14:38.963 --> 01:14:43.230
who are so severely underpaid
that we cannot really afford

01:14:43.230 --> 01:14:46.710
to be the consumer who chooses
between different things.

01:14:46.710 --> 01:14:51.090
And while it's true that
EU is doing certain things

01:14:51.090 --> 01:14:55.920
like AI act will regulate the control

01:14:55.920 --> 01:14:59.700
that retailers can have and
digital data, et cetera,

01:14:59.700 --> 01:15:02.160
but there are other norms that still,

01:15:02.160 --> 01:15:03.850
especially regarding consumption

01:15:05.424 --> 01:15:07.650
that have a very strong market bias

01:15:07.650 --> 01:15:11.640
and that are using, as a legal basis,

01:15:11.640 --> 01:15:14.610
the integration of the internal market,

01:15:14.610 --> 01:15:18.210
and that is pushing for
massive consumption.

01:15:18.210 --> 01:15:21.060
If you read even the new proposals

01:15:21.060 --> 01:15:23.520
on sustainable consumption,
it's almost ironic

01:15:23.520 --> 01:15:27.030
because the summaries and the recital says

01:15:27.030 --> 01:15:30.870
that having a variety of
products constantly circulating

01:15:30.870 --> 01:15:32.400
within the internal market

01:15:32.400 --> 01:15:35.250
is still Europe's number one priority.

01:15:35.250 --> 01:15:38.310
So that pushes towards
this type of consumption.

01:15:38.310 --> 01:15:43.170
So I'm curious to know
whether you're positive

01:15:43.170 --> 01:15:45.060
as what will happen to you

01:15:45.060 --> 01:15:48.363
or whether we are coming closer to you.

01:15:50.553 --> 01:15:53.790
- It's certainly the case
that, yeah, absolutely.

01:15:53.790 --> 01:15:56.520
There are certainly trends
that I signaled this,

01:15:56.520 --> 01:15:58.260
but I didn't maybe talk enough about it.

01:15:58.260 --> 01:15:59.670
There's certainly trends in Europe

01:15:59.670 --> 01:16:03.510
that are sort of converging
on the American model

01:16:03.510 --> 01:16:06.723
of consumption in some ways.

01:16:07.860 --> 01:16:10.800
I will say, and there are
certain ways in which,

01:16:10.800 --> 01:16:12.360
you know, some of these constraints

01:16:12.360 --> 01:16:14.790
that I was talking about,
like shopping hours,

01:16:14.790 --> 01:16:16.560
I mean when it comes to e-commerce,

01:16:16.560 --> 01:16:18.600
shopping hours is like imaginal line

01:16:18.600 --> 01:16:21.000
that is, like, long since
been breached, right?

01:16:21.000 --> 01:16:24.210
There's no limit as to when and how

01:16:24.210 --> 01:16:26.433
and where people can shop, right?

01:16:28.253 --> 01:16:30.240
And so I absolutely take your point.

01:16:30.240 --> 01:16:32.520
I do think that there has
been some convergence.

01:16:32.520 --> 01:16:37.170
I do still see though, that
there are bigger differences

01:16:37.170 --> 01:16:39.480
in terms of union coverage,

01:16:39.480 --> 01:16:41.880
even, you know, like, no other country,

01:16:41.880 --> 01:16:46.880
in no other country do
workers have to organize

01:16:47.250 --> 01:16:49.470
on a firm by firm basis, right?

01:16:49.470 --> 01:16:54.470
You have more encompassing
collective bargaining agreements,

01:16:54.810 --> 01:16:56.670
you have a stronger welfare state.

01:16:56.670 --> 01:17:01.080
And so, you know, there's a
more solid floor, I would say,

01:17:01.080 --> 01:17:02.670
partly because the reservation wage.

01:17:02.670 --> 01:17:05.160
You just can't pay people,
you know, two cents

01:17:05.160 --> 01:17:09.630
in order to perform certain tasks,

01:17:09.630 --> 01:17:11.400
where the floor in the United States,

01:17:11.400 --> 01:17:13.920
so far at least, has been just much lower.

01:17:13.920 --> 01:17:15.510
But I absolutely take your point,

01:17:15.510 --> 01:17:17.730
And, you know, I don't
mean to say that Europe

01:17:17.730 --> 01:17:21.750
hasn't developed a very
vibrant consumer culture.

01:17:21.750 --> 01:17:23.880
I also see that, you know, discounters

01:17:23.880 --> 01:17:28.320
have made inroads in Europe, absolutely.

01:17:28.320 --> 01:17:30.420
Many of them are actually,

01:17:30.420 --> 01:17:32.940
they took their lessons
from the United States,

01:17:32.940 --> 01:17:37.787
like Carrefour, which is the
biggest discounter in France,

01:17:39.540 --> 01:17:41.190
was founded by this guy

01:17:41.190 --> 01:17:43.230
who had just come from the United States,

01:17:43.230 --> 01:17:47.820
you know, having just
freshly completed an MBA

01:17:47.820 --> 01:17:50.973
and went and opened Carrefour.

01:17:52.140 --> 01:17:56.310
You also have hard discounters
in countries like Germany

01:17:56.310 --> 01:18:00.583
that have spread, especially
in the food sector.

01:18:02.970 --> 01:18:06.750
So Aldi, Lidl, these are these
so-called hard discounters

01:18:06.750 --> 01:18:09.480
that are just really low prices,

01:18:09.480 --> 01:18:11.640
also virtually no staffing.

01:18:11.640 --> 01:18:14.310
I mean, Trader Joe's actually belongs to,

01:18:14.310 --> 01:18:15.480
it was bought by Aldi.

01:18:15.480 --> 01:18:18.480
So, you know, if you were
shopping at Trader Joe's,

01:18:18.480 --> 01:18:21.903
that's actually an Aldi,

01:18:22.860 --> 01:18:24.750
extension of Aldi.

01:18:24.750 --> 01:18:28.230
So I absolutely think that
that's definitely going on.

01:18:28.230 --> 01:18:31.860
So far, it has been in certain sectors,

01:18:31.860 --> 01:18:34.050
you know, much more intense
than in other sectors.

01:18:34.050 --> 01:18:35.310
I would say, in the grocery sector,

01:18:35.310 --> 01:18:37.293
it's been especially intense.

01:18:38.130 --> 01:18:39.900
What maybe is different about the US

01:18:39.900 --> 01:18:44.360
is that it's so spread across
all product categories,

01:18:46.140 --> 01:18:47.073
so to speak.

01:18:52.320 --> 01:18:54.570
- Should I, anybody?

01:18:54.570 --> 01:18:55.906
- [Woman] Students?
- Oh yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:18:55.906 --> 01:18:59.535
- Professors.

01:18:59.535 --> 01:19:00.870
Ian.

01:19:00.870 --> 01:19:03.720
- Yeah, so I found this
really fascinating.

01:19:03.720 --> 01:19:07.743
I just wonder if isn't a
missing piece in the sort of,

01:19:10.044 --> 01:19:14.430
that goes to uniqueness of
the Amazon business model,

01:19:14.430 --> 01:19:17.040
which is, my reading the history

01:19:17.040 --> 01:19:22.040
is sort of the big box stores,
the Walmarts and the Sears

01:19:22.320 --> 01:19:27.320
and the Woolworths, they
put out neighborhood stores

01:19:28.650 --> 01:19:32.790
and they also curtailed a
lot of small manufacturing

01:19:32.790 --> 01:19:34.440
and small services.

01:19:34.440 --> 01:19:36.360
So, you know, the
neighborhood hardware store

01:19:36.360 --> 01:19:40.170
would also fix people's
shoes and cut keys,

01:19:40.170 --> 01:19:43.503
all of that stuff went to
the Walmarts and so on.

01:19:44.400 --> 01:19:48.300
And Amazon is putting
the big box stores out

01:19:48.300 --> 01:19:49.380
of business, right?

01:19:49.380 --> 01:19:52.560
I mean it's the Toys"R"Us, it's the Sears,

01:19:52.560 --> 01:19:56.280
those are the people that
are being shut down by Amazon

01:19:56.280 --> 01:19:59.500
because they are strictly retail

01:20:02.020 --> 01:20:04.830
and you see it where
they go global as well.

01:20:04.830 --> 01:20:07.020
So if you look at, like, India,

01:20:07.020 --> 01:20:09.540
where Walmart tried to go into India,

01:20:09.540 --> 01:20:14.163
they ran into a lot of
opposition from very small,

01:20:16.950 --> 01:20:18.450
you know, suppliers

01:20:18.450 --> 01:20:23.450
and suppliers of retail
services and manufacturing.

01:20:24.600 --> 01:20:28.800
Amazon is a huge success
in India very quickly

01:20:28.800 --> 01:20:32.190
because they're almost
like, you know, eBay there.

01:20:32.190 --> 01:20:36.810
I mean all these little suppliers
can just sell on Amazon.

01:20:36.810 --> 01:20:41.810
And so it does seem to
me, it's a different,

01:20:42.300 --> 01:20:45.570
maybe this is just yet
another stage or something,

01:20:45.570 --> 01:20:48.360
but it does seem like there
was this piece not there

01:20:48.360 --> 01:20:49.193
in your talk.

01:20:49.193 --> 01:20:50.680
So what you have to say about that?

01:20:50.680 --> 01:20:52.740
- Yeah, so I should have talked more

01:20:52.740 --> 01:20:55.340
about Amazon since it was
so prominent in the title.

01:20:57.240 --> 01:20:59.340
So that's the piece that I've,

01:20:59.340 --> 01:21:01.623
everybody I think knows,

01:21:02.463 --> 01:21:04.920
but in fact to link it up is important.

01:21:04.920 --> 01:21:07.950
First of all, just Walmart
also famously flamed out

01:21:07.950 --> 01:21:11.853
of the German market and
partly because of labor,

01:21:14.550 --> 01:21:15.840
they weren't cooperating with labor

01:21:15.840 --> 01:21:18.600
and labor wasn't gonna
cooperate with them.

01:21:18.600 --> 01:21:20.820
Yeah, so Walmart, I mean Amazon

01:21:20.820 --> 01:21:23.790
is the next phase of this, so to speak.

01:21:23.790 --> 01:21:26.820
And one of the things
that I try to point out

01:21:26.820 --> 01:21:28.470
is that there's a way in which,

01:21:28.470 --> 01:21:29.920
you know, people think that Amazon is,

01:21:29.920 --> 01:21:32.100
like, this whole new phenomenon.

01:21:32.100 --> 01:21:36.300
And there are certain ways
in which what Amazon is doing

01:21:36.300 --> 01:21:39.990
is totally different from
these previous big box stores.

01:21:39.990 --> 01:21:42.480
Like, big box stores, they're big,

01:21:42.480 --> 01:21:45.390
but ultimately the selection is limited

01:21:45.390 --> 01:21:48.150
to the amount of shelf
space that they have.

01:21:48.150 --> 01:21:52.560
Amazon has no such boundaries, right?

01:21:52.560 --> 01:21:53.393
That's one thing.

01:21:53.393 --> 01:21:54.567
The second thing is that-

01:21:54.567 --> 01:21:56.880
- [Man] No walk-in.
- And no walk-in.

01:21:56.880 --> 01:21:58.020
Well, that's the other thing.

01:21:58.020 --> 01:21:59.790
It makes purchasing frictionless.

01:21:59.790 --> 01:22:01.200
It's totally frictionless,

01:22:01.200 --> 01:22:05.700
especially if you get your
product in the buy box,

01:22:05.700 --> 01:22:07.470
then literally if you press the button,

01:22:07.470 --> 01:22:09.693
then the product is on the way.

01:22:10.620 --> 01:22:13.470
It also is very distinctive
in the sense that, you know,

01:22:13.470 --> 01:22:15.630
in order to get into these big box stores,

01:22:15.630 --> 01:22:19.590
your product already has
to be popular, right?

01:22:19.590 --> 01:22:21.810
You can't get space on an Amazon shelf

01:22:21.810 --> 01:22:23.853
unless you already have a following.

01:22:25.560 --> 01:22:28.200
Whereas in Amazon, it's sort of,

01:22:28.200 --> 01:22:31.050
what they call long tail retailing.

01:22:31.050 --> 01:22:34.020
It's like they could sell
even the most obscure things,

01:22:34.020 --> 01:22:37.560
the most niche product
that only one person wants.

01:22:37.560 --> 01:22:41.910
They can, you know, sell used
things right along new things.

01:22:41.910 --> 01:22:44.220
So in all those ways,

01:22:44.220 --> 01:22:46.560
there's something really
quite new about Amazon,

01:22:46.560 --> 01:22:51.560
but what I also am trying to
emphasize in this book, really,

01:22:51.750 --> 01:22:54.690
is that there's so much of this strategy

01:22:54.690 --> 01:22:57.513
that really draws on a very old playbook,

01:22:58.530 --> 01:23:01.140
an old playbook in terms
of the business strategy,

01:23:01.140 --> 01:23:03.810
thin, you know, low margins,

01:23:03.810 --> 01:23:07.380
thin, you know, sort of high volume sales,

01:23:07.380 --> 01:23:11.130
and also the political playbook that,

01:23:11.130 --> 01:23:13.290
you know, the way that
they rose to dominance.

01:23:13.290 --> 01:23:14.730
And so what I didn't tell here,

01:23:14.730 --> 01:23:17.370
but what, you know, is
definitely worth telling

01:23:17.370 --> 01:23:20.130
and is talked about in the book

01:23:20.130 --> 01:23:23.790
is how Amazon itself
used regulatory arbitrage

01:23:23.790 --> 01:23:25.950
in order to sort of grow to scale.

01:23:25.950 --> 01:23:29.130
So, like, Walmart grew very methodically,

01:23:29.130 --> 01:23:31.110
you know, he found, you know,

01:23:31.110 --> 01:23:33.900
Sam Walton founded a store
here and then he would,

01:23:33.900 --> 01:23:36.030
you know, circle that
store with a few others

01:23:36.030 --> 01:23:37.740
and kind of build out from there.

01:23:37.740 --> 01:23:39.630
The Amazon model was completely different.

01:23:39.630 --> 01:23:41.670
It was much closer to Amazon, you know,

01:23:41.670 --> 01:23:44.550
just scale up as fast as possible,

01:23:44.550 --> 01:23:46.380
you know, blanket the country

01:23:46.380 --> 01:23:50.370
and profitless growth for a long time.

01:23:50.370 --> 01:23:52.830
But a lot of it is
really the old playbook.

01:23:52.830 --> 01:23:56.780
It's like labor, you know,
labor law violations,

01:23:58.050 --> 01:24:02.703
low wages, a lot of
temporary contract workers,

01:24:03.870 --> 01:24:07.080
you know, a lot of regulatory
arbitrage in the early years.

01:24:07.080 --> 01:24:10.650
One of Amazon's huge
advantages was that they,

01:24:10.650 --> 01:24:12.660
you know, got around state tax laws

01:24:12.660 --> 01:24:16.980
by locating in countries or
in states that had low taxes

01:24:16.980 --> 01:24:21.150
and then shipping to places
that had higher taxes.

01:24:21.150 --> 01:24:24.390
I mean, the Supreme Court
finally closed that loophole,

01:24:24.390 --> 01:24:26.610
but sort of, like, the story
that I'm telling, you know,

01:24:26.610 --> 01:24:27.720
it was way later.

01:24:27.720 --> 01:24:30.090
By that time Amazon had grown to scale

01:24:30.090 --> 01:24:32.640
and by that time they had, you know,

01:24:32.640 --> 01:24:35.070
a lot of people who had come

01:24:35.070 --> 01:24:39.450
to organize their shopping
habits around this retailer.

01:24:39.450 --> 01:24:42.033
So I do wanna say that there is something,

01:24:43.080 --> 01:24:45.990
that Amazon is not
something out of brand new

01:24:45.990 --> 01:24:48.600
and the technology is
interesting and everything,

01:24:48.600 --> 01:24:51.870
but a lot of what they're doing
is really kind of goes back

01:24:51.870 --> 01:24:53.880
to old strategies.

01:24:53.880 --> 01:24:57.060
And I mean, you know, like,
Sears back in the day,

01:24:57.060 --> 01:24:58.980
they had the high tech warehouses.

01:24:58.980 --> 01:25:00.360
And what was high tech at that time?

01:25:00.360 --> 01:25:02.580
Well, it was pneumatic tubes that sent,

01:25:02.580 --> 01:25:05.520
you know, messages around the factory

01:25:05.520 --> 01:25:09.600
and now it's robots and pickers.

01:25:09.600 --> 01:25:12.880
And so I wanna kind of play around

01:25:13.770 --> 01:25:16.680
with the strand that really, you know,

01:25:16.680 --> 01:25:20.460
how we got to this kind
of crescendo of Amazon.

01:25:20.460 --> 01:25:23.460
And who knows if Amazon
is the last, you know,

01:25:23.460 --> 01:25:24.783
chapter in the story?

01:25:29.160 --> 01:25:31.920
- [Woman] Yeah, so thank you for coming,

01:25:31.920 --> 01:25:34.980
really engaged in talking,
excited to read the book,

01:25:34.980 --> 01:25:38.970
but I was really interested in this point

01:25:38.970 --> 01:25:41.970
about retailers exploiting
public infrastructure

01:25:41.970 --> 01:25:43.590
for private gains.

01:25:43.590 --> 01:25:46.290
And specifically two questions,

01:25:46.290 --> 01:25:50.740
one, pertaining to what
aspects about the US

01:25:52.320 --> 01:25:55.680
made these large
retailers able to exploit,

01:25:55.680 --> 01:25:57.843
like, USPS services,

01:25:59.880 --> 01:26:02.210
like, more so than retailers
in other countries?

01:26:02.210 --> 01:26:06.180
Is it just, like, a consequence of,

01:26:06.180 --> 01:26:07.950
like, US being a much larger country

01:26:07.950 --> 01:26:12.090
with, like, a greater proportion
of the population in rural.

01:26:12.090 --> 01:26:16.050
And then secondly, how in
the internet age specifically

01:26:16.050 --> 01:26:17.520
are retailers continuing

01:26:17.520 --> 01:26:20.010
to exploit this public infrastructure

01:26:20.010 --> 01:26:22.296
for their own personal gain?

01:26:22.296 --> 01:26:24.093
- Oh, great questions.

01:26:24.927 --> 01:26:29.927
So take the postal service question first.

01:26:31.830 --> 01:26:34.380
So these cooperatives that I
was talking about in Europe,

01:26:34.380 --> 01:26:37.110
they often also had credit
unions attached to them.

01:26:37.110 --> 01:26:39.900
And those credit unions were
a source of capital also

01:26:39.900 --> 01:26:41.790
to grow the co-ops.

01:26:41.790 --> 01:26:43.140
Credit unions in this country

01:26:43.140 --> 01:26:47.070
were basically boxed out until 1921,

01:26:47.070 --> 01:26:49.230
was the first time you
got any credit union

01:26:49.230 --> 01:26:52.110
at the state level, and
it was in Massachusetts.

01:26:52.110 --> 01:26:55.830
And they were boxed out by state bankers,

01:26:55.830 --> 01:26:58.320
state level bankers who
didn't want the competition.

01:26:58.320 --> 01:27:03.210
And so the US Postal
system was kind of became,

01:27:03.210 --> 01:27:05.190
you know, it wasn't
designed for that purpose,

01:27:05.190 --> 01:27:08.170
but that was gonna sort
of serve the same function

01:27:10.685 --> 01:27:11.550
as the cooperatives

01:27:11.550 --> 01:27:14.790
would've had already kind
of in-house at that time.

01:27:14.790 --> 01:27:18.150
And how did they continue to
exploit public infrastructure?

01:27:18.150 --> 01:27:21.450
I would say a lot of ways.

01:27:21.450 --> 01:27:26.340
One is just this dependence on
food stamps and SNAP benefits

01:27:26.340 --> 01:27:29.463
and the dependence on public,

01:27:31.140 --> 01:27:33.360
social benefits, right?

01:27:33.360 --> 01:27:37.770
That underwrites a low wage economy.

01:27:37.770 --> 01:27:40.410
You don't have to pay
workers if they, you know,

01:27:40.410 --> 01:27:44.010
you don't have to provide benefits,

01:27:44.010 --> 01:27:46.140
it sort of lowers the floor.

01:27:46.140 --> 01:27:50.293
And those low wages are,
in a fact, subsidized by,

01:27:54.960 --> 01:27:56.970
by public social benefits.

01:27:56.970 --> 01:27:58.620
And the other way is, you know,

01:27:58.620 --> 01:28:02.400
like, just the infrastructure of the,

01:28:02.400 --> 01:28:03.510
you know, the entire infrastructure,

01:28:03.510 --> 01:28:07.890
like, Walmart, for example,
depends enormously on,

01:28:07.890 --> 01:28:09.540
you know, trucking, right?

01:28:09.540 --> 01:28:12.270
And they were huge beneficiaries

01:28:12.270 --> 01:28:14.400
of the deregulation of trucking.

01:28:14.400 --> 01:28:17.100
Again, it was something
that they didn't cause,

01:28:17.100 --> 01:28:20.970
but they certainly promoted
the deregulation of trucking,

01:28:20.970 --> 01:28:23.940
which used to be a very unionized,

01:28:23.940 --> 01:28:26.250
a unionized sector.

01:28:26.250 --> 01:28:30.150
And that over time became
completely deregulated,

01:28:30.150 --> 01:28:32.970
such that the salaries of truckers

01:28:32.970 --> 01:28:36.960
are now really basically
subsistence wage salaries.

01:28:36.960 --> 01:28:40.020
And so, you know, the public
infrastructure, you know,

01:28:40.020 --> 01:28:43.230
Walmart depends entirely on the highways

01:28:43.230 --> 01:28:45.630
and these truckers that are
delivering all these goods,

01:28:45.630 --> 01:28:50.190
or independent contractors
who are dependent on,

01:28:50.190 --> 01:28:52.920
you know, sort of these
government programs

01:28:52.920 --> 01:28:54.660
to support their families.

01:28:54.660 --> 01:28:57.663
So I mean those are
just a few of the ways.

01:28:59.465 --> 01:29:03.340
- [Woman] We have time
for me one more question.

01:29:06.300 --> 01:29:10.437
- [Man] So I haven't shopped
on Amazon since 2017,

01:29:10.437 --> 01:29:12.177
when I read Lina Khan's piece,

01:29:12.177 --> 01:29:17.010
and I agree that consumer
choice is not the way out of it,

01:29:17.010 --> 01:29:19.740
this problem, but I was wondering,

01:29:19.740 --> 01:29:23.520
when you were talking
about kind of solutions,

01:29:23.520 --> 01:29:24.723
it seems to me,

01:29:27.600 --> 01:29:30.089
part of the thought in the talk

01:29:30.089 --> 01:29:33.900
is that there's a difference
between cooperation

01:29:33.900 --> 01:29:37.080
and concentration in antitrust regulation

01:29:37.080 --> 01:29:38.283
that might be relevant.

01:29:40.740 --> 01:29:42.793
I guess I just know American antitrust law

01:29:42.793 --> 01:29:46.020
is a kind of foreign distinction to me,

01:29:46.020 --> 01:29:48.450
and I'm was trying to
wrap my head around it.

01:29:48.450 --> 01:29:52.078
And it seems to me one way
that I might understand it,

01:29:52.078 --> 01:29:57.078
is that price competition
is maybe not what we want

01:29:57.150 --> 01:30:02.150
in a market so much as quality competition

01:30:03.570 --> 01:30:06.484
and innovation competition,
which is why it's okay

01:30:06.484 --> 01:30:10.080
for a bunch of small
producers to get together

01:30:10.080 --> 01:30:14.490
to maybe keep prices higher
than they would otherwise be,

01:30:14.490 --> 01:30:17.220
but they're gonna compete on other ways.

01:30:17.220 --> 01:30:20.523
I suspect that is maybe
not a distortion of the way

01:30:20.523 --> 01:30:23.400
that you would think about
it, but I guess I'm curious

01:30:23.400 --> 01:30:26.190
because largely, how do you think

01:30:26.190 --> 01:30:31.190
about differentiating
cooperation and concentration,

01:30:32.764 --> 01:30:33.603
and is there some kind of,

01:30:33.603 --> 01:30:38.190
do we need to stop thinking
about prices in the economy

01:30:38.190 --> 01:30:41.843
as a way of measuring whether
we're doing things better

01:30:41.843 --> 01:30:44.370
or building a better economy?

01:30:44.370 --> 01:30:45.870
- So that's a great question.

01:30:45.870 --> 01:30:47.160
It's a complicated question,

01:30:47.160 --> 01:30:49.653
but the short answer is,

01:30:50.610 --> 01:30:52.770
prices, yeah, shouldn't
be the only metric,

01:30:52.770 --> 01:30:54.297
because when you think
about consumer welfare,

01:30:54.297 --> 01:30:56.760
you could even think about,
you know, innovation,

01:30:56.760 --> 01:30:57.930
quality, et cetera.

01:30:57.930 --> 01:30:59.839
And these are all things

01:30:59.839 --> 01:31:01.860
that are valued differently
in other countries.

01:31:01.860 --> 01:31:04.890
I will say, and I have a paper
on this if you want more,

01:31:04.890 --> 01:31:07.710
but, you know, there is a
really important difference

01:31:07.710 --> 01:31:12.710
between, antitrust breaks out
into two things just crudely

01:31:13.560 --> 01:31:15.600
that are pretty distinct.

01:31:15.600 --> 01:31:18.910
One is, like, you know,
what is the attitude

01:31:20.914 --> 01:31:22.320
toward vertical concentration,

01:31:22.320 --> 01:31:25.530
on mergers and things like
that, and vertical dominance

01:31:25.530 --> 01:31:29.460
or the ability of big powerful companies

01:31:29.460 --> 01:31:33.390
to dominate more, you
know, sort of those players

01:31:33.390 --> 01:31:35.190
that are reliant on them?

01:31:35.190 --> 01:31:37.897
And then there's horizontal coordination.

01:31:41.880 --> 01:31:44.940
In general, and I'm gonna
sort of say this very crudely,

01:31:44.940 --> 01:31:46.470
but it's more complicated.

01:31:46.470 --> 01:31:48.810
In general, American antitrust

01:31:48.810 --> 01:31:53.810
has always been extremely
strict against any form

01:31:54.150 --> 01:31:56.400
of horizontal coordination.

01:31:56.400 --> 01:31:59.220
That's where Europe has
always been different

01:31:59.220 --> 01:32:01.680
and it remains different despite the fact

01:32:01.680 --> 01:32:04.050
that European antitrust

01:32:04.050 --> 01:32:07.530
has evolved a little bit
in the direction of the US.

01:32:07.530 --> 01:32:12.530
On many forms of coordination
among non-dominant players

01:32:12.660 --> 01:32:16.020
are seen as competition enhancing rather

01:32:16.020 --> 01:32:19.140
than, per se, illegal, okay?

01:32:19.140 --> 01:32:22.140
Vertical, I mean the US has
always been kind of forbearing

01:32:22.140 --> 01:32:24.810
on vertical restraints, right?

01:32:24.810 --> 01:32:26.940
And so now all of the attention

01:32:26.940 --> 01:32:30.210
or a lot of the attention in
sort of revising antitrust law

01:32:30.210 --> 01:32:33.720
in this country, I think it's good

01:32:33.720 --> 01:32:36.630
but it focuses almost exclusively

01:32:36.630 --> 01:32:39.300
on the issue of abusive dominance.

01:32:39.300 --> 01:32:42.240
And I think that's absolutely
a half of the picture,

01:32:42.240 --> 01:32:44.220
but the other half of the picture

01:32:44.220 --> 01:32:48.780
would be to talk about
greater forbearance vis-a-vis

01:32:48.780 --> 01:32:52.290
in terms of coordination
among non-dominant players,

01:32:52.290 --> 01:32:55.350
because I think that is
actually really important

01:32:55.350 --> 01:32:56.640
unless you think that the government

01:32:56.640 --> 01:32:58.620
can do all of this on its own.

01:32:58.620 --> 01:33:02.580
I really think that the
growing countervailing forces

01:33:02.580 --> 01:33:04.140
is really quite crucial

01:33:04.140 --> 01:33:08.853
to kind of sustaining
competition over the longer run.

01:33:10.200 --> 01:33:11.520
But there's more to be said.

01:33:11.520 --> 01:33:14.035
- Thank you so much, Professor Thelen,

01:33:14.035 --> 01:33:17.542
for this insightful lecture,
and everyone, for attending.

01:33:17.542 --> 01:33:20.542
(audience applauds)

01:33:24.967 --> 01:33:27.550
(bright music)

